window.__OPENSTAX_PHYSICS__[19] = {"schemaVersion":"4.0.0-physics","generatedAt":"2026-11-27T01:44:27.453Z","number":19,"code":"ch19","slug":"electrical-circuits","title":"Electrical Circuits","titleVi":"Mạch Điện Một Chiều & Định Luật Ohm (Electrical Circuits)","sections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec00","moduleId":"m54450","number":"19.0","slug":"introduction","title":"Introduction","isIntroduction":true,"learningObjectives":[],"bilingualGuide":null,"keyEquations":[],"subsections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec00.sub01","title":"Chapter Introduction","summaryVi":"Tổng quan mở đầu chương: Mạch Điện Một Chiều & Định Luật Ohm (Electrical Circuits) — giới thiệu hiện tượng thực tế và các câu hỏi nền tảng trước khi đi vào định luật chi tiết.","paragraphs":["The flicker of numbers on a handheld calculator, nerve impulses carrying signals of vision to the brain, an ultrasound device sending a signal to a computer screen, the brain sending a message for a baby to twitch its toes, an electric train pulling into a station, a hydroelectric plant sending energy to metropolitan and rural users—these and many other examples of electricity involve electric current, which is the movement of charge. Humanity has harnessed electricity, the basis of this technology, to improve our quality of life. Whereas the previous chapter concentrated on static electricity and the fundamental force underlying its behavior, the next two chapters will be devoted to electric and magnetic phenomena involving current. In addition to exploring applications of electricity, we shall gain new insights into the workings of nature."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167063727445","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_00_Hydro_N.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_00_Hydro_N.jpg","alt":"Water is shown flowing through a hydroelectric facility."}],"tables":[]}],"workedExamples":[],"snapLabs":[],"tipsAndFeatures":[],"summaryPoints":[],"glossary":[],"assessments":[],"stats":{"learningObjectiveCount":0,"keyEquationCount":0,"subsectionCount":1,"workedExampleCount":0,"snapLabCount":0,"glossaryCount":0,"assessmentCount":0}},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01","moduleId":"m54437","number":"19.1","slug":"ohm-s-law","title":"Ohm's law","isIntroduction":false,"learningObjectives":["Describe how current is related to charge and time, and distinguish between direct current and alternating current","Define resistance and verbally describe Ohm’s law","Calculate current and solve problems involving Ohm’s law"],"bilingualGuide":{"notebookSummaryVi":"Cường độ dòng điện (I = ΔQ/Δt), chiều dòng điện quy ước, phân biệt DC vs AC và Định luật Ohm (V = IR).","wordProblemCues":[{"phraseEn":"Ohm’s law","meaningVi":"Định luật Ohm: V = I·R","mathNotation":"I = V / R"}],"commonTrapsVi":["Chiều dòng điện quy ước đi từ (+) sang (−), ngược chiều chuyển động của các hạt electron!"]},"keyEquations":[],"subsections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.sub01","title":"Direct and Alternating Current","summaryVi":"Cường độ dòng điện (I = ΔQ/Δt), chiều dòng điện quy ước, phân biệt DC vs AC và Định luật Ohm (V = IR). Thuật ngữ trọng tâm trong mục này: alternating current (Dòng điện xoay chiều (AC)), ampere (Ampe (A)), conventional current (Chiều dòng điện quy ước), direct current (Dòng điện một chiều (DC)).","paragraphs":["Just as water flows from high to low elevation, electrons that are free to move will travel from a place with low potential to a place with high potential. A battery has two terminals that are at different potentials. If the terminals are connected by a conducting wire, an electric current (charges) will flow, as shown in Figure 19.1. Electrons will then move from the low-potential terminal of the battery (the negative end) through the wire and enter the high-potential terminal of the battery (the positive end).","Electric current is the rate at which electric charge moves. A large current, such as that used to start a truck engine, moves a large amount very quickly, whereas a small current, such as that used to operate a hand-held calculator, moves a small amount of charge more slowly. In equation form, electric current I is defined as","where \\(\\Delta Q\\) is the amount of charge that flows past a given area and \\(\\Delta t\\) is the time it takes for the charge to move past the area. The SI unit for electric current is the ampere (A), which is named in honor of the French physicist André-Marie Ampère (1775–1836). One ampere is one coulomb per second, or","Electric current moving through a wire is in many ways similar to water current moving through a pipe. To define the flow of water through a pipe, we can count the water molecules that flow past a given section of the pipe. As shown in Figure 19.1, electric current is very similar. We count the number of electrical charges that flow past a section of a conductor; in this case, a wire.","Assume each particle q in Figure 19.1 carries a charge \\(q = 1\\,\\text{nC}\\), in which case the total charge shown would be \\(\\Delta Q = 5q = 5\\,\\text{nC}\\) . If these charges move past the area A in a time \\(\\Delta t = 1\\,\\text{ns}\\), then the current would be","Note that we assigned a positive charge to the charges in Figure 19.1. Normally, negative charges—electrons—are the mobile charge in wires, as indicated in Figure 19.1. Positive charges are normally stuck in place in solids and cannot move freely. However, because a positive current moving to the right is the same as a negative current of equal magnitude moving to the left, as shown in Figure 19.1, we define conventional current to flow in the direction that a positive charge would flow if it could move. Thus, unless otherwise specified, an electric current is assumed to be composed of positive charges.","Also note that one Coulomb is a significant amount of electric charge, so 5 A is a very large current. Most often you will see current on the order of milliamperes (mA).","The direction of conventional current is the direction that positive charge would flow . Depending on the situation, positive charges, negative charges, or both may move. In metal wires, as we have seen, current is carried by electrons, so the negative charges move. In ionic solutions, such as salt water, both positively charged and negatively charged ions move. This is also true in nerve cells. Pure positive currents are relatively rare but do occur. History credits American politician and scientist Benjamin Franklin with describing current as the direction that positive charges flow through a wire. He named the type of charge associated with electrons negative long before they were known to carry current in so many situations.","As electrons move through a metal wire, they encounter obstacles such as other electrons, atoms, impurities, etc. The electrons scatter from these obstacles, as depicted in Figure 19.1. Normally, the electrons lose energy with each interaction. This energy is transferred to the wire and becomes thermal energy, which is what makes wires hot when they carry a lot of current. To keep the electrons moving thus requires a force, which is supplied by an electric field. The electric field in a wire points from the end of the wire at the higher potential to the end of the wire at the lower potential. Electrons, carrying a negative charge, move on average (or drift ) in the direction opposite the electric field, as shown in Figure 19.1.","So far, we have discussed current that moves constantly in a single direction. This is called direct current , because the electric charge flows in only one direction. Direct current is often called DC current.","Many sources of electrical power, such as the hydroelectric dam shown at the beginning of this chapter, produce alternating current , in which the current direction alternates back and forth. Alternating current is often called AC current . Alternating current moves back and forth at regular time intervals, as shown in Figure 19.1. The alternating current that comes from a normal wall socket does not suddenly switch directions. Rather, it increases smoothly up to a maximum current and then smoothly decreases back to zero. It then grows again, but in the opposite direction until it has reached the same maximum value. After that, it decreases smoothly back to zero, and the cycle starts over again.","Devices that use AC include vacuum cleaners, fans, power tools, hair dryers, and countless others. These devices obtain the power they require when you plug them into a wall socket. The wall socket is connected to the power grid that provides an alternating potential (AC potential). When your device is plugged in, the AC potential pushes charges back and forth in the circuit of the device, creating an alternating current.","Many devices, however, use DC, such as computers, cell phones, flashlights, and cars. One source of DC is a battery, which provides a constant potential (DC potential) between its terminals. With your device connected to a battery, the DC potential pushes charge in one direction through the circuit of your device, creating a DC current. Another way to produce DC current is by using a transformer, which converts AC potential to DC potential. Small transformers that you can plug into a wall socket are used to charge up your laptop, cell phone, or other electronic device. People generally call this a charger or a battery , but it is a transformer that transforms AC voltage into DC voltage. The next time someone asks to borrow your laptop charger, tell them that you don’t have a laptop charger, but that they may borrow your converter."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067275394","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_battery.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_battery.jpg","alt":"Electrons move from the negative terminal to the positive terminal of a battery."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167063690432","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_microcur.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_microcur.jpg","alt":"Positive charges move through a section of wire of cross-section A."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067107448","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_equivcur.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_equivcur.jpg","alt":"In part (a), positive charges move in the same direction as the current and electric field. In part (b), negative charges move against the direction of the current and electric field."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067056556","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_escatter.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_escatter.jpg","alt":"The random path and collisions of an electron moving in an electrical conductor opposite the current and electric field."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167066804876","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_accurrent.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_accurrent.jpg","alt":"A graph showing alternating current in which the current increases and decreases at regular intervals, and the direction of the current changing from one direction to another (arrows pointing left or right)"}],"tables":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.sub02","title":"Resistance and Ohm’s Law","summaryVi":"Cường độ dòng điện (I = ΔQ/Δt), chiều dòng điện quy ước, phân biệt DC vs AC và Định luật Ohm (V = IR). Thuật ngữ trọng tâm trong mục này: ampere (Ampe (A)), ohmic (Tuân theo Định luật Ohm (Tuyến tính)), Ohm’s law (Định luật Ohm (I = V/R)), resistance (Điện trở (R)).","paragraphs":["As mentioned previously, electrical current in a wire is in many ways similar to water flowing through a pipe. The water current that can flow through a pipe is affected by obstacles in the pipe, such as clogs and narrow sections in the pipe. These obstacles slow down the flow of current through the pipe. Similarly, electrical current in a wire can be slowed down by many factors, including impurities in the metal of the wire or collisions between the charges in the material. These factors create a resistance to the electrical current. Resistance is a description of how much a wire or other electrical component opposes the flow of charge through it.\nIn the 19th century, the German physicist Georg Simon Ohm (1787–1854) found experimentally that current through a conductor is proportional to the voltage drop across a current-carrying conductor.","The constant of proportionality is the resistance R of the material, which leads to","This relationship is called Ohm’s law . It can be viewed as a cause-and-effect relationship, with voltage being the cause and the current being the effect. Ohm’s law is an empirical law like that for friction, which means that it is an experimentally observed phenomenon. The units of resistance are volts per ampere, or V/A. We call a V/A an ohm , which is represented by the uppercase Greek letter omega ( \\(\\Omega\\) ). Thus,","Ohm’s law holds for most materials and at common temperatures. At very low temperatures, resistance may drop to zero (superconductivity). At very high temperatures, the thermal motion of atoms in the material inhibits the flow of electrons, increasing the resistance. The many substances for which Ohm’s law holds are called ohmic . Ohmic materials include good conductors like copper, aluminum, and silver, and some poor conductors under certain circumstances. The resistance of ohmic materials remains essentially the same for a wide range of voltage and current.","This simulation mimics a simple circuit with batteries providing the voltage source and a resistor connected across the batteries. See how the current is affected by modifying the resistance and/or the voltage. Note that the resistance is modeled as an element containing small scattering centers . These represent impurities or other obstacles that impede the passage of the current."],"figures":[],"tables":[]}],"workedExamples":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.ex01","number":"19.1.1","title":"Current in a Lightning Strike","problem":"A lightning strike can transfer as many as \\({10}^{20}\\) electrons from the cloud to the ground. If the strike lasts 2 ms, what is the average electric current in the lightning?","problemImages":[],"strategy":"Use the definition of current, \\(I = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}\\) . The charge \\(\\Delta Q\\)\nfrom \\({10}^{20}\\) electrons is \\(\\Delta Q = ne\\), where \\(n = {10}^{20}\\) is the number of electrons and \\(e = - 1.60\\, \\times \\,{10}^{- 19}\\text{C}\\) is the charge on the electron. This gives\n\\(\\Delta Q = {10}^{20}\\, \\times \\,(-1.60\\, \\times \\,{10}^{-19}\\text{C}) = -16.0\\,\\text{C.}\\)\nThe time \\(\\Delta t = 2\\, \\times \\,{10}^{-3}\\text{s}\\) is the duration of the lightning strike.","solution":"The current in the lightning strike is\n\\(\\begin{aligned} I = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t} \\\\ = \\frac{-16.0\\,\\text{C}}{2\\, \\times \\,{10}^{-3}\\text{s}} \\\\ = -8\\,\\text{kA.} \\end{aligned}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"Discussion\nThe negative sign reflects the fact that electrons carry the negative charge. Thus, although the electrons flow from the cloud to the ground, the positive current is defined to flow from the ground to the cloud."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.ex02","number":"19.1.2","title":"Average Current to Charge a Capacitor","problem":"In a circuit containing a capacitor and a resistor, it takes 1 min to charge a 16 μF capacitor by using a 9-V battery. What is the average current during this time?","problemImages":[],"strategy":"We can determine the charge on the capacitor by using the definition of capacitance: \\(C = \\frac{Q}{V}\\) . When the capacitor is charged by a 9-V battery, the voltage across the capacitor will be \\(V = 9\\,\\text{V}\\) . This gives a charge of\n\\(\\begin{aligned} C = \\frac{Q}{V} \\\\ Q = CV\\text{.} \\end{aligned}\\)\nBy inserting this expression for charge into the equation for current, \\(I = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}\\), we can find the average current.","solution":"The average current is\n\\(\\begin{aligned} I = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t} \\\\ = \\frac{CV}{\\Delta t} \\\\ = \\frac{(16\\, \\times \\,{10}^{-6}\\text{F})(9\\,\\text{V})}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\ = 2.4\\, \\times \\,{10}^{-6}\\text{A} \\\\ = 2.4\\,\\text{μA.} \\end{aligned}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"Discussion\nThis small current is typical of the current encountered in circuits such as this."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.ex03","number":"19.1.3","title":"Resistance of a Headlight","problem":"What is the resistance of an automobile headlight through which 2.50 A flows when 12.0 V is applied to it?","problemImages":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167063526032","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_headlight_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_headlight_img.jpg","alt":"A headlight is connected to a battery with current flowing from the positive terminal of the battery into the headlight and returning to the battery via the negative terminal."}],"strategy":"Ohm’s law tells us \\({V}_{\\text{headlight}} = I{R}_{\\text{headlight}}\\) . The voltage drop in going through the headlight is just the voltage rise supplied by the battery, \\({V}_{\\text{headlight}} = {V}_{\\text{battery}}\\) . We can use this equation and rearrange Ohm’s law to find the resistance \\({R}_{\\text{headlight}}\\) of the headlight.","solution":"Solving Ohm’s law for the resistance of the headlight gives\n\\(\\begin{aligned} {V}_{\\text{headlight}} = I{R}_{\\text{headlight}} \\\\ {V}_{\\text{battery}} = I{R}_{\\text{headlight}} \\\\ {R}_{\\text{headlight}} = \\frac{{V}_{\\text{battery}}}{I} = \\frac{12\\,\\text{V}}{2.5\\,\\text{A}} = 4.8\\,\\text{Ω.} \\end{aligned}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"Discussion\nThis is a relatively small resistance. As we will see below, resistances in circuits are commonly measured in kΩ or MΩ."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.ex04","number":"19.1.4","title":"Determine Resistance from Current-Voltage Graph","problem":"Suppose you apply several different voltages across a circuit and measure the current that runs through the circuit. A plot of your results is shown in Figure 19.1. What is the resistance of the circuit?\nThe line shows the current as a function of voltage. Notice that the current is given in milliamperes. For example, at 3 V, the current is 0.003 A, or 3 mA.","problemImages":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067010238","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_IVgraph.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_01_IVgraph.jpg","alt":"The graph shows current in milliamperes on the y-axis and voltage in volts on the x-axis with a straight line such that at 3V, the current is 3 mA."}],"strategy":"The plot shows that current is proportional to voltage, which is Ohm’s law. In Ohm’s law ( \\(V = IR\\) ), the constant of proportionality is the resistance R . Because the graph shows current as a function of voltage, we have to rearrange Ohm’s law in that form: \\(I = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{1}{R}\\, \\times \\,V\\) . This shows that the slope of the line of I versus V is \\(\\frac{1}{R}\\) . Thus, if we find the slope of the line in Figure 19.1, we can calculate the resistance R .","solution":"The slope of the line is the rise divided by the run . Looking at the lower-left square of the grid, we see that the line rises by 1 mA (0.001 A) and runs over a voltage of 1 V. Thus, the slope of the line is\n\\(\\text{slope} = \\frac{0.001\\,\\text{A}}{1\\,\\text{V.}}\\)\nEquating the slope with \\(\\frac{1}{R}\\) and solving for R gives\n\\(\\begin{aligned} \\frac{1}{R} = \\frac{0.001\\,\\text{A}}{1} \\\\ R = \\frac{1\\,\\text{V}}{0.001\\,\\text{A}} = 1,000\\,\\Omega \\end{aligned}\\)\nor 1 k-ohm.","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"Discussion\nThis resistance is greater than what we found in the previous example. Resistances such as this are common in electric circuits, as we will discover in the next section. Note that if the line in Figure 19.1 were not straight, then the material would not be ohmic and we would not be able to use Ohm’s law. Materials that do not follow Ohm’s law are called nonohmic ."}],"snapLabs":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.lab01","title":"Vegetable Current","text":"This lab helps students understand how current works. Given that particles confined in a pipe cannot occupy the same space, pushing more particles into one end of the pipe will force the same number of particles out of the opposite end. This creates a current of particles.\nFind a straw and dried peas that can move freely in the straw. Place the straw flat on a table and fill the straw with peas. When you push one pea in at one end, a different pea should come out of the other end. This demonstration is a model for an electric current. Identify the part of the model that represents electrons and the part of the model that represents the supply of electrical energy. For a period of 30 s, count the number of peas you can push through the straw. When finished, calculate the pea current by dividing the number of peas by the time in seconds.\nNote that the flow of peas is based on the peas physically bumping into each other; electrons push each other along due to mutually repulsive electrostatic forces.","images":[]}],"tipsAndFeatures":[],"summaryPoints":["Direct current is constant over time; alternating current alternates smoothly back and forth over time.","Electrical resistance causes materials to extract work from the current that flows through them.","In ohmic materials, voltage drop along a path is proportional to the current that runs through the path."],"glossary":[{"term":"alternating current","termVi":"Dòng điện xoay chiều (AC)","ipa":"/ˈɒl.tə.neɪ.tɪŋ/","definition":"electric current whose direction alternates back and forth at regular intervals","definitionVi":"Dòng điện đổi chiều tuần hoàn theo thời gian (như điện lưới gia đình 220V – 50Hz ở Việt Nam, 120V – 60Hz ở Mỹ).","memoryTipVi":"Dễ dàng tăng/hạ áp bằng máy biến áp."},{"term":"ampere","termVi":"Ampe (A)","ipa":"/ˈæm.peər/","definition":"unit for electric current; one ampere is one coulomb per second ( \\(1\\,\\text{A} = 1\\,\\text{C/s}\\) )","definitionVi":"Đơn vị SI đo cường độ dòng điện (1 A = 1 C/s).","memoryTipVi":"Đo bằng Ampe kế mắc NỐI TIẾP."},{"term":"conventional current","termVi":"Chiều dòng điện quy ước","ipa":"/kənˈven.ʃən.əl/","definition":"flows in the direction that a positive charge would flow if it could move","definitionVi":"Quy ước là chiều dịch chuyển của các điện tích DƯƠNG (từ cực + sang cực −), ngược chiều dịch chuyển thực tế của electron!","memoryTipVi":"⚠️ Chiều dòng điện I ngược với chiều chạy của electron tự do!"},{"term":"direct current","termVi":"Dòng điện một chiều (DC)","ipa":"/daɪˈrekt ˈkʌr.ənt/","definition":"electric current that flows in a single direction","definitionVi":"Dòng điện chỉ chạy theo một chiều cố định (như dòng điện từ pin, ắc quy).","memoryTipVi":"Pin điện thoại, ắc quy dùng DC."},{"term":"electric current","termVi":"Cường độ dòng điện (I = ΔQ/Δt)","ipa":"/ɪˈlek.trɪk ˈkʌr.ənt/","definition":"electric charge that is moving","definitionVi":"Lượng điện tích dịch chuyển có hướng qua tiết diện dây dẫn trong 1 giây (đơn vị Ampe: 1 A = 1 C/s).","memoryTipVi":"I = ΔQ / Δt."},{"term":"nonohmic","termVi":"Phi tuyến (Không tuân theo Định luật Ohm)","ipa":"/nɒnˈəʊ.mɪk/","definition":"material that does not follow Ohm’s law","definitionVi":"Linh kiện có điện trở thay đổi theo dòng điện/nhiệt độ (như bóng đèn dây tóc nóng sáng, đi-ốt bán dẫn).","memoryTipVi":"Đồ thị I–V là đường cong."},{"term":"ohmic","termVi":"Tuân theo Định luật Ohm (Tuyến tính)","ipa":"/ˈəʊ.mɪk/","definition":"material that obeys Ohm’s law","definitionVi":"Linh kiện có điện trở R không đổi, đồ thị I–V là đường thẳng đi qua gốc tọa độ.","memoryTipVi":"Điện trở kim loại ở nhiệt độ không đổi là Ohmic."},{"term":"Ohm’s law","termVi":"Định luật Ohm (I = V/R)","ipa":"/əʊmz lɔː/","definition":"electric current is proportional to the voltage applied across a circuit or other path","definitionVi":"Cường độ dòng điện qua vật dẫn tỉ lệ thuận với hiệu điện thế và tỉ lệ nghịch với điện trở: V = I·R.","memoryTipVi":"Công thức nền tảng nhất của mạch điện!"},{"term":"resistance","termVi":"Điện trở (R)","ipa":"/rɪˈzɪs.təns/","definition":"how much a circuit element opposes the passage of electric current; it appears as the constant of proportionality in Ohm’s law","definitionVi":"Đại lượng đặc trưng cho mức độ cản trở dòng điện của vật dẫn (đơn vị Ohm, Ω): R = ρL / A.","memoryTipVi":"Dây càng dài (L lớn) và càng mảnh (A nhỏ) thì điện trở R càng lớn."}],"assessments":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.q004","sourceId":"fs-id1167063667117","category":"practice-problems","categoryLabel":"Practice Problems (Bài tập vận dụng)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"10 nC of charge flows through a circuit in 3.0 × 10 -6 s . What is the current during this time?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The current passes through the circuit is 3.3 × 10 -3 A."},{"label":"b","text":"The current passes through the circuit is 30 A."},{"label":"c","text":"The current passes through the circuit is 33 A."},{"label":"d","text":"The current passes through the circuit is 0.3 A."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.q012","sourceId":"fs-id1167067203601","category":"critical-thinking","categoryLabel":"Critical Thinking (Tư duy phản biện)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"An accelerator accelerates He nuclei (charge = 2 e ) to a speed of v = 2 × 10 6 m/s. What is the current if the linear density of He nuclei is λ = 10 8 m –1 ?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"I = 9.6 × 10 –5 A"},{"label":"b","text":"I = 3.2 × 10 –5 A"},{"label":"c","text":"I = 12.8 × 10 –5 A"},{"label":"d","text":"I = 6.4 × 10 –5 A"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.q014","sourceId":"fs-id1167063667117","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"10 nC of charge flows through a circuit in 3.0 × 10 -6 s . What is the current during this time?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The current passes through the circuit is 3.3 × 10 -3 A."},{"label":"b","text":"The current passes through the circuit is 30 A."},{"label":"c","text":"The current passes through the circuit is 33 A."},{"label":"d","text":"The current passes through the circuit is 0.3 A."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.q018","sourceId":"fs-id1167066778836","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What voltage is needed to make 6 C of charge traverse a 100-Ω resistor in 1 min?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The required voltage is 1 × 10 -3 V."},{"label":"b","text":"The required voltage is 10 V."},{"label":"c","text":"The required voltage is 1,000 V."},{"label":"d","text":"The required voltage is 10,000 V."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.q019","sourceId":"fs-id1163727300699","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Resistors typically obey Ohm’s law at low currents, but show deviations at higher currents because of heating. Suppose you were to conduct an experiment measuring the voltage, V , across a resistor as a function of current, I , including currents whose deviations from Ohms law start to become apparent. For a data plot of V versus I , which of the following functions would be best to fit the data? Assume that a, b , and c are nonzero constants adjusted to fit the data.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"\\(V = aI\\)"},{"label":"b","text":"\\(V = aI + b\\)"},{"label":"c","text":"\\(V = aI + b{I}^{2}\\)"},{"label":"d","text":"\\(V = aI + b{I}^{2} + c\\)"}],"hasOfficialSolution":true,"solution":"The correct answer is (c). Choice (a) can be eliminated because it does not show deviations from Ohm’s Law. Choices (b) and (d) can be eliminated because the voltage drop across the resistor would be nonzero when the current is zero, which is inconsistent with the fact that Ohm’s Law is obeyed at low currents. Choice (c) follows Ohm’s Law at low currents because the linear term will dominate the quadratic term, with deviations from Ohm’s Law becoming apparent at higher currents."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.q023","sourceId":"fs-id1167067239854","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"The equivalent unit for an ohm is a ________.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"V/A"},{"label":"b","text":"C/m"},{"label":"c","text":"\\(\\frac{\\text{A}}{\\text{V}}\\)"},{"label":"d","text":"V/s"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.q025","sourceId":"fs-id1167061217589","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"True or false—it is possible to produce nonzero DC current by adding together AC currents.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"false"},{"label":"b","text":"true"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.q026","sourceId":"fs-id1167061209143","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What type of current is used in cars?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"alternating current"},{"label":"b","text":"indirect current"},{"label":"c","text":"direct current"},{"label":"d","text":"straight current"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec01.q029","sourceId":"fs-id1167063800786","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the current through a 100-Ω resistor with 12 V across it?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"0"},{"label":"b","text":"0.12 A"},{"label":"c","text":"8.33 A"},{"label":"d","text":"1,200 A"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null}],"stats":{"learningObjectiveCount":3,"keyEquationCount":0,"subsectionCount":2,"workedExampleCount":4,"snapLabCount":1,"glossaryCount":9,"assessmentCount":9}},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02","moduleId":"m54435","number":"19.2","slug":"series-circuits","title":"Series Circuits","isIntroduction":false,"learningObjectives":["Interpret circuit diagrams and diagram basic circuit elements","Calculate equivalent resistance of resistors in series and apply Ohm’s law to resistors in series and apply Ohm’s law to resistors in series"],"bilingualGuide":{"notebookSummaryVi":"Đoạn mạch mắc nối tiếp (Series Circuits): I như nhau ở mọi điểm, V = V₁ + V₂ + ..., R_eq = R₁ + R₂ + ...","wordProblemCues":[{"phraseEn":"connected in series","meaningVi":"Mắc nối tiếp (cùng dòng điện I)","mathNotation":"R_eq = ΣR_i"}],"commonTrapsVi":["Trong mạch nối tiếp, chỉ cần 1 bóng đèn bị cháy đứt dây tóc là CẢ MẠCH tắt hết!"]},"keyEquations":[{"name":"the equivalent resistance of N resistors connected in series","formula":"\\({R}_{\\text{equiv}} = {R}_{1} + {R}_{2} + ⋯ + {R}_{N}\\)"}],"subsections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.sub01","title":"Electric Circuits and Resistors","summaryVi":"Đoạn mạch mắc nối tiếp (Series Circuits): I như nhau ở mọi điểm, V = V₁ + V₂ + ..., R_eq = R₁ + R₂ + ... Thuật ngữ trọng tâm trong mục này: circuit diagram (Sơ đồ mạch điện), electric circuit (Mạch điện kín), resistor (Linh kiện điện trở), steady state (Trạng thái xác lập (ổn định)).","paragraphs":["Now that we understand the concept of electric current, let’s see what we can do with it. As you are no doubt aware, the modern lifestyle relies heavily on electrical devices. These devices contain ingenious electric circuits , which are complete, closed pathways through which electric current flows. Returning to our water analogy, an electric circuit is to electric charge like a network of pipes is to water: The electric circuit guides electric charge from one point to the next, running the charge through various devices along the way to extract work or information.","Electric circuits are made from many materials and cover a huge range of sizes, as shown in Figure 19.2. Computers and cell phones contain electric circuits whose features can be as small as roughly a billionth of a meter (a nanometer, or \\({10}^{-9}\\text{m}\\) ). The pathways that guide the current in these devices are made by ultraprecise chemical treatments of silicon or other semiconductors. Large power systems, on the other hand, contain electric circuits whose features are on the scale of meters. These systems carry such large electric currents that their physical dimensions must be relatively large.","The pathways that form electric circuits are made from a conducting material, normally a metal in macroscopic circuits. For example, copper wires inside your school building form the electrical circuits that power lighting, projectors, screens, speakers, etc. To represent an electric circuit, we draw circuit diagrams . We use lines and symbols to represent the elements in the circuit. A simple electric circuit diagram is shown on the left side of Figure 19.2. On the right side is an analogous water circuit, which we discuss below.","There are many different symbols that scientists and engineers use in circuit diagrams, but we will focus on four main symbols: the wire, the battery or voltage source, resistors, and the ground. The thin black lines in the electric circuit diagram represent the pathway that the electric charge must follow. These pathways are assumed to be perfect conductors, so electric charge can move along these pathways without losing any energy. In reality, the wires in circuits are not perfect, but they come close enough for our purposes.","The zigzag element labeled R is a resistor , which is a circuit element that provides a known resistance. Macroscopic resistors are often color coded to indicate their resistance, as shown in Figure 19.2.","The red element in Figure 19.2 is a battery, with its positive and negative terminals indicated; the longer line represents the positive terminal of the battery, and the shorter line represents the negative terminal. Note that the battery icon is not always colored red; this is done in Figure 19.2 just to make it easy to identify.","Finally, the element labeled ground on the lower left of the circuit indicates that the circuit is connected to Earth, which is a large, essentially neutral object containing an infinite amount of charge. Among other things, the ground determines the potential of the negative terminal of the battery. Normally, the potential of the ground is defined to be zero: \\({V}_{\\text{ground}}≡0\\) . This means that the entire lower wire in Figure 19.2 is at a voltage of zero volts.","The electric current in Figure 19.2 is indicted by the blue line labeled I . The arrow indicates the direction in which positive charge would flow in this circuit. Recall that, in metals, electrons are mobile charge carriers, so negative charges actually flow in the opposite direction around this circuit (i.e., counterclockwise). However, we draw the current to show the direction in which positive charge would move.","On the right side of Figure 19.2 is an analogous water circuit. Water at a higher pressure leaves the top of the pump, which is like charges leaving the positive terminal of the battery. The water travels through the pipe, like the charges traveling through the wire. Next, the water goes through a sand filter, which heats up as the water squeezes through. This step is like the charges going through the resistor. When charges flow through a resistor, they do work to heat up the resistor. After flowing through the sand filter, the water has converted its potential energy into heat, so it is at a lower pressure. Likewise, the charges exiting the resistor have converted their potential energy into heat, so they are at a lower voltage. Recall that voltage is just potential energy per charge. Thus, water pressure is analogous to electric potential energy (i.e., voltage). Coming back to the water circuit again, we see that the water returns to the bottom of the pump, which is like the charge returning to the negative terminal of the battery. The water pump uses a source of energy to pump the water back up to a high pressure again, giving it the pressure required to go through the circuit once more. The water pump is like the battery, which uses chemical energy to increase the voltage of the charge up to the level of the positive terminal.","The potential energy per charge at the positive terminal of the battery is the voltage rating of the battery. This voltage is like water pressure in the upper pipe. Just like a higher pressure forces water to move toward a lower pressure, a higher voltage forces electric charge to flow toward a lower voltage. The pump takes water at low pressure and does work on it, ejecting water at a higher pressure. Likewise, a battery takes charge at a low voltage, does work on it, and ejects charge at a higher voltage.","Note that the current in the water circuit of Figure 19.2 is the same throughout the circuit. In other words, if we measured the number of water molecules passing a cross-section of the pipe per unit time at any point in the circuit, we would get the same answer no matter where in the circuit we measured. The same is true of the electrical circuit in the same figure. The electric current is the same at all points in this circuit, including inside the battery and in the resistor. The electric current neither speeds up in the wires nor slows down in the resistor. This would create points where too much or too little charge would be bunched up. Thus, the current is the same at all points in the circuit shown in Figure 19.2.","Although the current is the same everywhere in both the electric and water circuits, the voltage or water pressure changes as you move through the circuits. In the water circuit, the water pressure at the pump outlet stays the same until the water goes through the sand filter, assuming no energy loss in the pipe. Likewise, the voltage in the electrical circuit is the same at all points in a given wire, because we have assumed that the wires are perfect conductors. Thus, as indicated by the constant red color of the upper wire in Figure 19.2, the voltage throughout this wire is constant at \\(V = {V}_{\\text{battery}}\\) . The voltage then drops as you go through the resistor, but once you reach the blue wire, the voltage stays at its new level of \\(V = 0\\) all the way to the negative terminal of the battery (i.e., the blue terminal of the battery).","If we go from the blue wire through the battery to the red wire, the voltage increases from \\(V = 0\\)\nto \\(V = {V}_{\\text{battery}}\\) . Likewise, if we go from the blue wire up through the resistor to the red wire, the voltage also goes from \\(V = 0\\) to \\(V = {V}_{\\text{battery}}\\) . Thus, using Ohm’s law, we can write","\\({V}_{\\text{resistor}} = {V}_{\\text{battery}} = IR\\text{.}\\)","Note that \\({V}_{\\text{resistor}}\\) is measured from the bottom of the resistor to the top, meaning that the top of the resistor is at a higher voltage than the bottom of the resistor. Thus, current flows from the top of the resistor or higher voltage to the bottom of the resistor or lower voltage.","Use this simulation to better understand how resistance, voltage, and current are related. The simulation shows a battery with a resistor connected between the terminals of the battery, as in the previous figure. You can modify the battery voltage and the resistance. The simulation shows how electrons react to these changes. It also shows the atomic cores in the resistor and how they are excited and heat up as more current goes through the resistor.","Draw the circuit diagram for the circuit, being sure to draw an arrow indicating the direction of the current. Now pick three spots along the wire. Without changing the settings, allow the simulation to run for 20 s while you count the number of electrons passing through that spot. Record the number on the circuit diagram. Now do the same thing at each of the other two spots in the circuit. What do you notice about the number of charges passing through each spot in 20 s? Remember that that current is defined as the rate that charges flow through the circuit. What does this mean about the current through the entire circuit?","Other possible circuit elements include capacitors and switches. These are drawn as shown on the left side of Figure 19.2. A switch is a device that opens and closes the circuit, like a light switch. It is analogous to a valve in a water circuit, as shown on the right side of Figure 19.2. With the switch open, no current passes through the circuit. With the switch closed, it becomes part of the wire, so the current passes through it with no loss of voltage.","The capacitor is labeled C on the left of Figure 19.2. A capacitor in an electrical circuit is analogous to a flexible membrane in a water circuit. When the switch is closed in the circuit of Figure 19.2, the battery forces electrical current to flow toward the capacitor, charging the upper capacitor plate with positive charge. As this happens, the voltage across the capacitor plates increases. This is like the membrane in the water circuit: When the valve is opened, the pump forces water to flow toward the membrane, making it stretch to store the excess water. As this happens, the pressure behind the membrane increases.","Now if we open the switch, the capacitor holds the voltage between its plates because the charges have nowhere to go. Likewise, if we close the valve, the water has nowhere to go and the membrane maintains the water pressure in the pipe between itself and the valve.","If the switch is closed for a long time in the electric circuit or if the valve is open for a long time in the water circuit, the current will eventually stop flowing because the capacitor or the membrane will have become completely charged. Each circuit is now in the steady state , which means that its characteristics do not change over time. In this case, the steady state is characterized by zero current, and this does not change as long as the switch or valve remains in the same position. In the steady state, no electrical current passes through the capacitor, and no water current passes through the membrane. The voltage difference between the capacitor plates will be the same as the battery voltage. In the water circuit, the pressure behind the membrane will be the same as the pressure created by the pump.","Although the circuit in Figure 19.2 may seem a bit pointless because all that happens when the switch is closed is that the capacitor charges up, it does show the capacitor’s ability to store charge. Thus, the capacitor serves as a reservoir for charge. This property of capacitors is used in circuits in many ways. For example, capacitors are used to power circuits while batteries are being charged. In addition, capacitors can serve as filters. To understand this, let’s go back to the water analogy. Suppose you have a water hose and are watering your garden. Your friend thinks he’s funny, and kinks the hose. While the hose is kinked, you experience no water flow. When he lets go, the water starts flowing again. If he does this really fast, you experience water-no water-water-no water, and that’s really no way to water your garden. Now imagine that the hose is filling up a big bucket, and you are watering from the bottom of the bucket. As long as you had water in your bucket to begin with and your friend doesn’t kink the water hose for too long, you would be able to water your garden without the interruptions. Your friend kinking the water hose is filtered by the big bucket’s supply of water, so it does not impact your ability to water the garden. We can think of the interruptions in the current (be it water or electrical current) as noise . Capacitors act in an analogous way as the water bucket to help filter out the noise. Capacitors have so many uses that it is very rare to find an electronic circuit that does not include some capacitors.","Amuse yourself by building circuits of all different shapes and sizes. This simulation provides you with various standard circuit elements, such as batteries, AC voltage sources, resistors, capacitors, light bulbs, switches, etc. You can connect these in any configuration you like and then see the result.","Build a circuit that starts with a resistor connected to a capacitor. Connect the free side of the resistor to the positive terminal of a battery and the free side of the capacitor to the negative terminal of the battery. Click the reset dynamics button to see how the current flows starting with no charge on the capacitor. Now right click on the resistor to change its value. When you increase the resistance, does the circuit reach the steady state more rapidly or more slowly?"],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065967352","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_circuits.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_circuits.jpg","alt":"Part (a) shows a circuit board with a small “chip.” Part (b) shows a high-power electric power transmission station."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167064564102","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_circuit01.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_circuit01.jpg","alt":"Part (a) shows a circuit diagram with a zigzag line of a resistor, a battery, and current. Part (b) shows an analogous water circuit with a pump (similar to a battery) and a sand filter (similar to the resistor)."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167066183260","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_resistors.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_resistors.jpg","alt":"Typical resistors of various color values are shown."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065874664","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_consvolt.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_consvolt.jpg","alt":"A circuit diagram showing a battery and resistor at constant voltage: V=Vbattery and V=Vground=0."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167064937973","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_circuit02.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_circuit02.jpg","alt":"Part (a) is a circuit diagram with a battery, switch, and capacitor. Part (b) is an analogous water circuit with a pump (like a battery), a valve (like a switch), and a stretchable membrane (like a capacitor)."}],"tables":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.sub02","title":"Resistors in Series and Equivalent Resistance","summaryVi":"Đoạn mạch mắc nối tiếp (Series Circuits): I như nhau ở mọi điểm, V = V₁ + V₂ + ..., R_eq = R₁ + R₂ + ... Thuật ngữ trọng tâm trong mục này: electric circuit (Mạch điện kín), equivalent resistor (Điện trở tương đương (R_eq)), in series (Mắc nối tiếp), resistor (Linh kiện điện trở).","paragraphs":["Now that we have a basic idea of how electrical circuits work, let’s see what happens in circuits with more than one circuit element. In this section, we look at resistors in series. Components connected in series are connected one after the other in the same branch of a circuit, such as the resistors connected in series on the left side of Figure 19.2.","We will now try to find a single resistance that is equivalent to the three resistors in series on the left side of Figure 19.2. An equivalent resistor is a resistor that has the same resistance as the combined resistance of a set of other resistors. In other words, the same current will flow through the left and right circuits in Figure 19.2 if we use the equivalent resistor in the right circuit.","According to Ohm’s law, the voltage drop V across a resistor when a current flows through it is \\(V = IR\\) where I is the current in amperes (A) and R is the resistance in ohms ( \\(\\Omega\\) ). Another way to think of this is that V is the voltage necessary to make a current I flow through a resistance R . Applying Ohm’s law to each resistor on the left circuit of Figure 19.2, we find that the voltage drop across \\({R}_{1}\\) is \\({V}_{1} = I{R}_{1}\\), that across \\({R}_{2}\\) is \\({V}_{2} = I{R}_{2}\\), and that across \\({R}_{3}\\) is \\({V}_{3} = I{R}_{3}\\) . The sum of these voltages equals the voltage output of the battery, that is","You may wonder why voltages must add up like this. One way to understand this is to go once around the circuit and add up the successive changes in voltage. If you do this around a loop and get back to the starting point, the total change in voltage should be zero, because you end up at the same place that you started. To better understand this, consider the analogy of going for a stroll through some hilly countryside. If you leave your car and walk around, then come back to your car, the total height you gained in your stroll must be the same as the total height you lost, because you end up at the same place as you started. Thus, the gravitational potential energy you gain must be the same as the gravitational potential energy you lose. The same reasoning holds for voltage in going around an electric circuit. Let’s apply this reasoning to the left circuit in Figure 19.2. We start just below the battery and move up through the battery, which contributes a voltage gain of \\({V}_{\\text{battery}}\\) . Next, we got through the resistors. The voltage drops by \\({V}_{1}\\) in going through resistor \\({R}_{1}\\), by \\({V}_{2}\\) in going through resistor \\({R}_{2}\\), and by \\({V}_{3}\\) in going through resistor \\({R}_{3}\\) . After going through resistor \\({R}_{3}\\), we arrive back at the starting point, so we add up these four changes in voltage and set the sum equal to zero. This gives","which is the same as the previous equation. Note that the minus signs in front of \\({V}_{\\text{1}}\\text{,}{V}_{\\text{2}}\\text{, and}{V}_{3}\\) are because these are voltage drops , whereas \\({V}_{\\text{battery}}\\) is a voltage rise .","Ohm’s law tells us that \\({V}_{1} = I{R}_{1}\\), \\({V}_{2} = I{R}_{2}\\), and \\({V}_{3} = I{R}_{3}\\) . Inserting these values into equation \\({V}_{\\text{battery}} = {V}_{1} + {V}_{2} + {V}_{3}\\) gives","Applying this same logic to the right circuit in\nFigure 19.2 gives","Dividing the equation \\({V}_{\\text{battery}} = I({R}_{1} + {R}_{2} + {R}_{3})\\) by \\({V}_{\\text{battery}} = I{R}_{\\text{equiv}}\\), we get","This shows that the equivalent resistance for a series of resistors is simply the sum of the resistances of each resistor. In general, N resistors connected in series can be replaced by an equivalent resistor with a resistance of"],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167062465567","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_circuit03.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_circuit03.jpg","alt":"The left circuit diagram shows three resistors and a battery connected in series. The right circuit diagram shows one resistor that is equivalent to the combination of the three resistors shown on the left."}],"tables":[]}],"workedExamples":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.ex01","number":"19.2.1","title":"Calculation of Equivalent Resistance","problem":"In the left circuit of the previous figure, suppose the voltage rating of the battery is 12 V, and the resistances are \\({R}_{1} = 1.0\\,\\Omega ,{R}_{2} = 6.0\\,\\Omega ,\\text{and}{R}_{3} = 13\\,\\Omega\\) . (a) What is the equivalent resistance? (b) What is the current through the circuit?\nStrategy FOR (A)\nUse the equation for the equivalent resistance of resistors connected in series. Because the circuit has three resistances, we only need to keep three terms, so it takes the form\n\\({R}_{\\text{equiv}} = {R}_{1} + {R}_{2} + {R}_{3}\\text{.}\\)","problemImages":[],"strategy":null,"solution":"Solution for (a)\nInserting the given resistances into the equation above gives\n\\(\\begin{aligned} {R}_{\\text{equiv}} = {R}_{1} + {R}_{2} + {R}_{3} \\\\ = 1.0\\,\\Omega + 6.0\\,\\Omega + 13\\,\\Omega \\\\ = 20\\,\\text{Ω.} \\end{aligned}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"We can thus replace the three resistors \\({R}_{1},{R}_{2},\\text{and}{R}_{3}\\) with a single 20- \\(\\Omega\\) resistor."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.ex02","number":"19.2.2","title":"Determine the Unknown Resistance","problem":"The circuit shown in figure below contains three resistors of known value and a third element whose resistance \\({R}_{3}\\) is unknown. Given that the equivalent resistance for the entire circuit is 150 \\(\\Omega\\), what is the resistance \\({R}_{3}\\) ?","problemImages":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065741679","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_BBresistor_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_BBresistor_img.jpg","alt":"A circuit diagram shows a battery and four resistors; only the resistances of three of the resistors are given."}],"strategy":"The four resistances in this circuit are connected in series, so we know that they must add up to give the equivalent resistance. We can use this to find the unknown resistance \\({R}_{3}\\)\n.","solution":"For four resistances in series, the equation for the equivalent resistance of resistors in series takes the form\n\\({R}_{\\text{equiv}} = {R}_{1} + {R}_{2} + {R}_{3} + {R}_{4}\\text{.}\\)\nSolving for R 3 and inserting the known values gives\n\\(\\begin{aligned} {R}_{3} = {R}_{\\text{equiv}} - {R}_{1} - {R}_{2} - {R}_{4} \\\\ = 150\\,\\Omega - 10\\,\\Omega - 25\\,\\Omega - 15\\,\\Omega \\\\ = 100\\,\\text{Ω.} \\end{aligned}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"The equivalent resistance of a circuit can be measured with an ohmmeter. This is sometimes useful for determining the effective resistance of elements whose resistance is not marked on the element."}],"snapLabs":[],"tipsAndFeatures":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.feat01","type":"physics-in-action","title":"What It Takes to be an Electrical Engineer","text":"Physics is used in a wide variety of fields. One field that requires a very thorough knowledge of physics is electrical engineering. An electrical engineer can work on anything from the large-scale power systems that provide power to big cities to the nanoscale electronic circuits that are found in computers and cell phones (Figure 19.2).\nIn working with power companies, you can be responsible for maintaining the power grid that supplies electrical power to large areas. Although much of this work is done from an office, it is common to be called in for overtime duty after storms or other natural events. Many electrical engineers enjoy this part of the job, which requires them to race around the countryside repairing high-voltage transformers and other equipment. However, one of the more unpleasant aspects of this work is to remove the carcasses of unfortunate squirrels or other animals that have wandered into the transformers.\nOther careers in electrical engineering can involve designing circuits for cell phones, which requires cramming some 10 billion transistors into an electronic chip the size of your thumbnail. These jobs can involve much work with computer simulations and can also involve fields other than electronics. For example, the 1-m-diameter lenses that are used to make these circuits (as of 2015) are so precise that they are shipped from the manufacture to the chip fabrication plant in temperature-controlled trucks to ensure that they are held within a certain temperature range. If they heat up or cool down too much, they deform ever so slightly, rendering them useless for the ultrahigh precision photolithography required to manufacture these chips.\nIn addition to a solid knowledge of physics, electrical engineers must above all be practical. Consider, for example, how one corporation managed to launch some anti-ballistic missiles at the White Sands Missile Test Range in New Mexico in the 1960s. Before launch, the skin of the missile had to be at the same voltage as the rail from which it was launched. The rail was connected to the ground by a large copper wire connected to a stake driven into the sandy earth. The missile, however, was connected by an umbilical cord to the equipment in the control shed a few meters away, which was grounded via a different grounding circuit. Before launching the missile, the voltage difference between the missile skin and the rail had to be less than 2.5 V. After an especially dry spell of weather, the missile could not be launched because the voltage difference stood at 5 V. A group of electrical engineers, including the father of your author, stood around pondering how to reduce the voltage difference. The situation was resolved when one of the engineers realized that urine contains electrolytes and conducts electricity quite well. With that, the four engineers quickly resolved the problem by urinating on the rail spike. The voltage difference immediately dropped to below 2.5 V and the missile was launched on schedule.\nThe systems that electrical engineers work on range from microprocessor circuits (left)] to missile systems (right).","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065824249","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_EE.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_EE.jpg","alt":"The left shows a microprocessor chip; the right shows a missile being launched."}]}],"summaryPoints":["Circuit diagrams are schematic representations of electric circuits.","Resistors in series are resistors that are connected head to tail.","The same current runs through all resistors in series; however, the voltage drop across each resistor can be different.","The voltage is the same at every point in a given wire."],"glossary":[{"term":"circuit diagram","termVi":"Sơ đồ mạch điện","ipa":"/ˈdaɪ.ə.ɡræm/","definition":"schematic drawing of an electrical circuit including all circuit elements, such as resistors, capacitors, batteries, and so on","definitionVi":"Hình vẽ biểu diễn mạch điện bằng các ký hiệu quy ước chuẩn (nguồn, điện trở, khóa K, tụ điện).","memoryTipVi":"Vạch dài trên ký hiệu pin là cực dương (+), vạch ngắn dày là cực âm (−)."},{"term":"electric circuit","termVi":"Mạch điện kín","ipa":"/ˈsɜː.kɪt/","definition":"physical network of paths through which electric current can flow","definitionVi":"Đường dẫn khép kín cho phép dòng điện chạy liên tục từ nguồn qua tải rồi về lại nguồn.","memoryTipVi":"Mạch hở (open circuit) → I = 0."},{"term":"equivalent resistor","termVi":"Điện trở tương đương (R_eq)","ipa":"/ɪˈkwɪv.ə.lənt/","definition":"resistance of a single resistor that is the same as the combined resistance of a group of resistors","definitionVi":"Một điện trở duy nhất có thể thay thế cả cụm điện trở mà giữ nguyên dòng điện và hiệu điện thế mạch chính.","memoryTipVi":"Nối tiếp: R_eq = R₁ + R₂; Song song: 1/R_eq = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂."},{"term":"in series","termVi":"Mắc nối tiếp","ipa":"/ɪn ˈsɪə.riːz/","definition":"when elements in a circuit are connected one after the other in the same branch of the circuit","definitionVi":"Cách mắc các linh kiện nối đuôi nhau trên cùng 1 nhánh duy nhất → CÙNG cường độ dòng điện I!","memoryTipVi":"Mắc nối tiếp: I bằng nhau, V cộng lại, R_eq tăng lên."},{"term":"resistor","termVi":"Linh kiện điện trở","ipa":"/rɪˈzɪs.tər/","definition":"circuit element that provides a known resistance","definitionVi":"Linh kiện điện tử được chế tạo với giá trị điện trở xác định để hạn chế dòng hoặc chia áp.","memoryTipVi":"Ký hiệu đường gấp khúc zíc-zắc (chuẩn Mỹ) hoặc hình chữ nhật (chuẩn IEC/VN)."},{"term":"steady state","termVi":"Trạng thái xác lập (ổn định)","ipa":"/ˈsted.i steɪt/","definition":"when the characteristics of a system do not change over time","definitionVi":"Trạng thái mạch điện khi dòng điện và điện áp ở mọi điểm không còn biến thiên theo thời gian.","memoryTipVi":"Khi tụ điện đã nạp đầy hoàn toàn trong mạch DC, nhánh chứa tụ coi như hở mạch."}],"assessments":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q001","sourceId":"fs-id1167066156473","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What circuit element is represented in the figure above?","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1164568073880","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_ex41_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_ex41_img.jpg","alt":"An image is shown with a continuous line. The line starts out straight and black, becomes red and up and down pointed, and goes back to straight and black."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"a battery"},{"label":"b","text":"a resistor"},{"label":"c","text":"a capacitor"},{"label":"d","text":"an inductor"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q003","sourceId":"fs-id1167065911680","category":"concept","categoryLabel":"Concept Items (Câu hỏi khái niệm)","isMultipleChoice":false,"prompt":"In which circuit are all the resistors connected in series?","images":[{"sourceId":"eip-idm365187904","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_series1_SM_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_series1_SM_img.jpg","alt":"Circuit has three vertical resistors connected in a line."},{"sourceId":"eip-idm212233440","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_series2_SM_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_series2_SM_img.jpg","alt":"Circuit has two vertical resistors connected in line and a third resistor connected across the line between the two resistors."},{"sourceId":"eip-idm216167456","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_series3_SM_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_series3_SM_img.jpg","alt":"Circuit has one resistor connected to a pair of two horizontal resistors, which are connected on both ends."},{"sourceId":"eip-idm216165968","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_series4_SM_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_series4_SM_img.jpg","alt":"Circuit has three resistors in a horizontal line, and all are connected on both ends."}],"choices":[],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q004","sourceId":"eip-id1166849370147","category":"concept","categoryLabel":"Concept Items (Câu hỏi khái niệm)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the voltage and current through the capacitor in the circuit below a long time after the switch is closed?","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1169831865779","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_opencircui_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_opencircui_img.jpg","alt":"The figure shows a circuit with an open switch with one end connected to a capacitor. The other end of the capacitor is connected to the negative terminal of a 10-volt voltage source. The positive terminal of the voltage source is connected to the other end of the open switch. The connection between the capacitor and voltage source is also connected to the ground."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"0 V, 0 A"},{"label":"b","text":"0 V, 10 A"},{"label":"c","text":"10 V, 0 A"},{"label":"d","text":"10 V, 10 A"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q005","sourceId":"fs-id1167066202869","category":"critical-thinking","categoryLabel":"Critical Thinking (Tư duy phản biện)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Given three batteries (5V, 9V, 12V) and five resistors (10, 20, 30, 40, 50Ω) to choose from, what can you choose to form a circuit diagram with a current of 0.175A? You do not need to use all of the components.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Batteries (5V, 9V) and resistors (30Ω, 50Ω) connected in series"},{"label":"b","text":"Batteries (5V4, 12V) and resistors (10Ω, 20Ω, 40Ω, and 50Ω) connected in series."},{"label":"c","text":"Batteries (5V, 9V, and 12V) and resistors (10Ω, 20Ω, and 30Ω) connected in series."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q007","sourceId":"eip-id1166847299978","category":"critical-thinking","categoryLabel":"Critical Thinking (Tư duy phản biện)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Rank the points A, B, C, and D in the circuit diagram from lowest voltage to highest voltage.","images":[{"sourceId":"eip-idm366511072","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_ABCDcircui_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_ABCDcircui_img.jpg","alt":"The figure shows a circuit with four points labeled A, B, D, and C in clockwise direction. Between points A and B is a voltage of 5 volts with the negative terminal connected to point A. Between points B and D is a resistor of 500 ohms. Between points D and C is a voltage of 24 volts with negative terminal connected to point D and ground. Finally, between points C and A is a resistor of 300 ohms."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"A, B, C, D"},{"label":"b","text":"B, C, A, D"},{"label":"c","text":"C, B, A, D"},{"label":"d","text":"D, A, B, C"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q008","sourceId":"fs-id1167062414094","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the voltage drop across two 80-Ω resistors connected in series with 0.15 A flowing through them?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"12 V"},{"label":"b","text":"24 V"},{"label":"c","text":"36 V"},{"label":"d","text":"48 V"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q009","sourceId":"fs-id1167064695191","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"In this circuit, the voltage drop across the upper resistor is 4.5 V. What is the battery voltage?","images":[{"sourceId":"eip-idm206570048","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_ex44_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_ex44_img.jpg","alt":"The figure shows a rectangular circuit with a battery and two resistors connected in series. The battery is located on the left-hand segment of the circuit, a 30-ohm resistor is located on the upper segment, and a 50-ohm resistor is located on the right-hand segment of the circuit."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"4.5V"},{"label":"b","text":"7.5V"},{"label":"c","text":"12V"},{"label":"d","text":"18V"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q010","sourceId":"fs-id1167065949186","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What does the circuit element shown represent?","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1164567119435","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_ex47_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_ex47_img.jpg","alt":"The figure shows a symbol with one vertical line connected to a horizontal line. The horizontal line has a short line under it, which in turn has another shorter line under it."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"a battery"},{"label":"b","text":"a capacitor"},{"label":"c","text":"the ground"},{"label":"d","text":"a switch"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q011","sourceId":"fs-id1167065894489","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"How many 10-Ω resistors must be connected in series to make an equivalent resistance of 80 Ω?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"80"},{"label":"b","text":"8"},{"label":"c","text":"20"},{"label":"d","text":"40"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q012","sourceId":"eip-id1166848792362","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Which two circuit elements are represented in the circuit diagram?","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1164564289171","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_capacresis_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_02_capacresis_img.jpg","alt":"The figure shows two circuit elements connected to each other. The first element is represented by two parallel lines, and the second element is represented by a wiggly line."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"a battery connected in series with an inductor"},{"label":"b","text":"a capacitor connected in series with a resistor"},{"label":"c","text":"a resistor connected in series with a battery"},{"label":"d","text":"an inductor connected in series with a resistor"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q013","sourceId":"eip-id1166851225351","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"How much current will flow through a 10-V battery with a 100-Ω resistor connected across its terminals?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"0.1 A"},{"label":"b","text":"1.0 A"},{"label":"c","text":"0"},{"label":"d","text":"1,000 A"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q014","sourceId":"fs-id1167065885455","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Given a circuit with one 9-V battery and with its negative terminal connected to ground. The two paths are connected to ground from the positive terminal: the right path with a 20-Ω and a 100-Ω resistor and the left path with a 50-Ω resistor. How much current will flow in the right branch?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"\\(\\frac{\\text{9}}{\\text{120}}\\)"},{"label":"b","text":"\\(\\frac{\\text{9}}{\\text{100}}\\)"},{"label":"c","text":"\\(\\frac{\\text{9}}{\\text{50}}\\)"},{"label":"d","text":"\\(\\frac{\\text{9}}{\\text{20}}\\)"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec02.q019","sourceId":"fs-id1167066142440","category":"test-prep-er","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Tự luận mở rộng","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the current through each resistor in the circuit?","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1169828589258","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex54_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex54_img.jpg","alt":"The figure shows a circuit with five vertical branches connected at the top and bottom. The bottom is also connected to ground. From left to right, the branch elements are 50-ohm R 1, 80-ohm R 2, 24-volt voltage source, 20-ohm R 3, and 100-ohm R 4, respectively."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Current through resistors R 1 , R 2 , R 3 , and R 4 is 0.48 A, 0.30 A, 1.2 A, and 0.24 A, respectively."},{"label":"b","text":"Current through resistors R 1 , R 2 , R 3 , and R 4 is 1200 A, 1920 A, 480 A, and 2400 A, respectively."},{"label":"c","text":"Current through resistors R 1 , R 2 , R 3 , and is R 4 2.08 A, 3.34 A, 0.833 A, and 4.17 A, respectively."},{"label":"d","text":"The same amount of current, 0.096 A, flows through all of the resistors."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null}],"stats":{"learningObjectiveCount":2,"keyEquationCount":1,"subsectionCount":2,"workedExampleCount":2,"snapLabCount":0,"glossaryCount":6,"assessmentCount":13}},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03","moduleId":"m54442","number":"19.3","slug":"parallel-circuits","title":"Parallel Circuits","isIntroduction":false,"learningObjectives":["Interpret circuit diagrams with parallel resistors","Calculate equivalent resistance of resistor combinations containing series and parallel resistors"],"bilingualGuide":{"notebookSummaryVi":"Đoạn mạch mắc song song (Parallel Circuits): V như nhau ở mọi nhánh, I = I₁ + I₂ + ..., 1/R_eq = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + ...","wordProblemCues":[{"phraseEn":"connected in parallel","meaningVi":"Mắc song song (cùng điện áp V)","mathNotation":"1/R_eq = Σ(1/R_i)"}],"commonTrapsVi":["Sau khi tính được tổng 1/R₁ + 1/R₂, ĐỪNG QUÊN lấy nghịch đảo lại để ra R_eq!"]},"keyEquations":[{"name":"the equivalent resistance of N resistors connected in parallel","formula":"\\({R}_{\\text{equiv}} = \\frac{1}{1/{R}_{1} + 1/{R}_{2} + ⋯ + 1/{R}_{N}}\\)"}],"subsections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.sub01","title":"Resistors in Parallel","summaryVi":"Đoạn mạch mắc song song (Parallel Circuits): V như nhau ở mọi nhánh, I = I₁ + I₂ + ..., 1/R_eq = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + ... Thuật ngữ trọng tâm trong mục này: in parallel (Mắc song song).","paragraphs":["In the previous section, we learned that resistors in series are resistors that are connected one after the other. If we instead combine resistors by connecting them next to each other, as shown in Figure 19.3, then the resistors are said to be connected in parallel . Resistors are in parallel when both ends of each resistor are connected directly together.","Note that the tops of the resistors are all connected to the same wire, so the voltage at the top of the each resistor is the same. Likewise, the bottoms of the resistors are all connected to the same wire, so the voltage at the bottom of each resistor is the same. This means that the voltage drop across each resistor is the same. In this case, the voltage drop is the voltage rating V of the battery, because the top and bottom wires connect to the positive and negative terminals of the battery, respectively.","Although the voltage drop across each resistor is the same, we cannot say the same for the current running through each resistor. Thus, \\({I}_{1},{I}_{2},\\text{and}{I}_{3}\\) are not necessarily the same, because the resistors \\({R}_{1},{R}_{2},\\text{and}{R}_{3}\\) do not necessarily have the same resistance.","Note that the three resistors in Figure 19.3 provide three different paths through which the current can flow. This means that the equivalent resistance for these three resistors must be less than the smallest of the three resistors. To understand this, imagine that the smallest resistor is the only path through which the current can flow. Now add on the alternate paths by connecting other resistors in parallel. Because the current has more paths to go through, the overall resistance (i.e., the equivalent resistance) will decrease. Therefore, the equivalent resistance must be less than the smallest resistance of the parallel resistors.","To find the equivalent resistance \\({R}_{\\text{equiv}}\\) of the three resistors \\({R}_{1},{R}_{2},\\text{and}{R}_{3}\\), we apply Ohm’s law to each resistor. Because the voltage drop across each resistor is V , we obtain","or","We also know from conservation of charge that the three currents \\({I}_{1},{I}_{2},\\text{and}{I}_{3}\\) must add up to give the current I that goes through the battery. If this were not true, current would have to be mysteriously created or destroyed somewhere in the circuit, which is physically impossible. Thus, we have","Inserting the expressions for \\({I}_{1},{I}_{2},\\text{and}{I}_{3}\\) into this equation gives","or","This formula is just Ohm’s law, with the factor in parentheses being the equivalent resistance.","Thus, the equivalent resistance for three resistors in parallel is","The same logic works for any number of resistors in parallel, so the general form of the equation that gives the equivalent resistance of N resistors connected in parallel is"],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065763621","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit04.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit04.jpg","alt":"On the left is a circuit diagram with three resistors connected in parallel. On the right is a circuit diagram with only one resistor that has equivalent resistance to the three resistors shown on the left."}],"tables":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.sub02","title":"Resistors in Parallel and in Series","summaryVi":"Đoạn mạch mắc song song (Parallel Circuits): V như nhau ở mọi nhánh, I = I₁ + I₂ + ..., 1/R_eq = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + ... Thuật ngữ trọng tâm trong mục này: in parallel (Mắc song song).","paragraphs":["More complex connections of resistors are sometimes just combinations of series and parallel. Combinations of series and parallel resistors can be reduced to a single equivalent resistance by using the technique illustrated in Figure 19.3. Various parts are identified as either series or parallel, reduced to their equivalents, and further reduced until a single resistance is left. The process is more time consuming than difficult.","Let’s work through the four steps in Figure 19.3 to reduce the seven resistors to a single equivalent resistor. To avoid distracting algebra, we’ll assume each resistor is 10 \\(\\Omega\\) . In step 1, we reduce the two sets of parallel resistors circled by the blue dashed loop. The upper set has three resistors in parallel and will be reduced to a single equivalent resistor \\({R}_{\\text{P1}}\\) . The lower set has two resistors in parallel and will be reduced to a single equivalent resistor \\({R}_{\\text{P2}}\\) . Using the equation for the equivalent resistance of resistors in parallel, we obtain","These two equivalent resistances are encircled by the red dashed loop following step 1. They are in series, so we can use the equation for the equivalent resistance of resistors in series to reduce them to a single equivalent resistance \\({R}_{\\text{S1}}\\) . This is done in step 2, with the result being","The equivalent resistor \\({R}_{\\text{S1}}\\) appears in the green dashed loop following step 2. This resistor is in parallel with resistor \\({R}_{\\text{7}}\\), so the pair can be replaced by the equivalent resistor \\({R}_{\\text{P3}}\\), which is given by","This is done in step 3. The resistor \\({R}_{\\text{P3}}\\) is in series with the resistor \\({R}_{\\text{1}}\\), as shown in the purple dashed loop following step 3. These two resistors are combined in the final step to form the final equivalent resistor \\({R}_{\\text{equiv}}\\), which is","Thus, the entire combination of seven resistors may be replaced by a single resistor with a resistance of about 14.5 \\(\\Omega\\) .","That was a lot of work, and you might be asking why we do it. It’s important for us to know the equivalent resistance of the entire circuit so that we can calculate the current flowing through the circuit. Ohm’s law tells us that the current flowing through a circuit depends on the resistance of the circuit and the voltage across the circuit. But to know the current, we must first know the equivalent resistance.","Here is a general approach to find the equivalent resistor for any arbitrary combination of resistors:","Robots have captured our collective imagination for over a century. Now, this dream of creating clever machines to do our dirty work, or sometimes just to keep us company, is becoming a reality. Robotics has become a huge field of research and development, with some technology already being commercialized. Think of the small autonomous vacuum cleaners, for example.","Figure 19.3 shows just a few of the multitude of different forms robots can take. The most advanced humanoid robots can walk, pour drinks, even dance (albeit not very gracefully). Other robots are bio-inspired, such as the dogbot shown in the middle photograph of Figure 19.3. This robot can carry hundreds of pounds of load over rough terrain. The photograph on the right in Figure 19.3 shows the inner workings of an M-block, developed by the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. These simple-looking blocks contain inertial wheels and electromagnets that allow them to spin and flip into the air and snap together in a variety of shapes. By communicating wirelessly between themselves, they self-assemble into a variety of shapes, such as desks, chairs, and someday maybe even buildings.","All robots involve an immense amount of physics and engineering. The simple act of pouring a drink has only recently been mastered by robots, after over 30 years of research and development! The balance and timing that we humans take for granted is in fact a very tricky act to follow, requiring excellent balance, dexterity, and feedback. To master this requires sensors to detect balance, computing power to analyze the data and communicate the appropriate compensating actions, and joints and actuators to implement the required actions.","In addition to sensing gravity or acceleration, robots can contain multiple different sensors to detect light, sound, temperature, smell, taste, etc. These devices are all based on the physical principles that you are studying in this text. For example, the optics used for robotic vision are similar to those used in your digital cameras: pixelated semiconducting detectors in which light is converted into electrical signals. To detect temperature, simple thermistors may be used, which are resistors whose resistance changes depending on temperature.","Building a robot today is much less arduous than it was a few years ago. Numerous companies now offer kits for building robots. These range in complexity something suitable for elementary school children to something that would challenge the best professional engineers. If interested, you may find these easily on the Internet and start making your own robot today."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167064848441","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit05.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit05.jpg","alt":"Four steps are shown to simplify a complex circuit diagram of seven resistors to one with only a single equivalent resistor. Initially, two groups of parallel resistors, circled by the blue dashed loop, are combined; then, two resistors in series, circled by the red dashed loop, are combined, which is then combined with a resistor in parallel, circled by the green dashed loop; finally, two resistors in series are combined, circled by the purple dashed loop, yielding the final diagram."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065869566","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_robots.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_robots.jpg","alt":"A “dogbot” in the general shape of a dog is shown"}],"tables":[]}],"workedExamples":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.ex01","number":"19.3.1","title":"Find the Current through Parallel Resistors","problem":"The three circuits below are equivalent. If the voltage rating of the battery is \\({V}_{\\text{battery}} = 3\\,\\text{V}\\), what is the equivalent resistance of the circuit and what current runs through the circuit?","problemImages":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167066034121","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_Eqresex01_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_Eqresex01_img.jpg","alt":"Three equivalent circuit diagrams are shown, each with three resistors connected in parallel."}],"strategy":"The three resistors are connected in parallel and the voltage drop across them is V battery . Thus, we can apply the equation for the equivalent resistance of resistors in parallel, which takes the form\n\\({R}_{\\text{equiv}} = \\frac{1}{1/{R}_{1} + 1/{R}_{2} + 1/{R}_{3}}\\text{.}\\)\nThe circuit with the equivalent resistance is shown below. Once we know the equivalent resistance, we can use Ohm’s law to find the current in the circuit.","solution":"Inserting the given values for the resistance into the equation for equivalent resistance gives\n\\(\\begin{aligned} {R}_{\\text{equiv}} = \\frac{1}{1/{R}_{1} + 1/{R}_{2} + 1/{R}_{3}} \\\\ = \\frac{1}{1/10\\,\\Omega + 1/25\\,\\Omega + 1/15\\,\\Omega} \\\\ = 4.84\\,\\text{Ω.} \\end{aligned}\\) The current through the circuit is thus\n\\(\\begin{aligned} V = IR \\\\ I = \\frac{V}{R} \\\\ = \\frac{3\\,\\text{V}}{4.84\\,\\Omega} \\\\ = 0.62\\,\\text{A.} \\end{aligned}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"Although 0.62 A flows through the entire circuit, note that this current does not flow through each resistor. However, because electric charge must be conserved in a circuit, the sum of the currents going through each branch of the circuit must add up to the current going through the battery. In other words, we cannot magically create charge somewhere in the circuit and add this new charge to the current. Let’s check this reasoning by using Ohm’s law to find the current through each resistor.\n\\(\\begin{aligned} {I}_{1} = \\frac{V}{{R}_{1}} = \\frac{3\\,\\text{V}}{10\\,\\Omega} = 0.30\\,\\text{A} \\\\ {I}_{2} = \\frac{V}{{R}_{2}} = \\frac{3\\,\\text{V}}{25\\,\\Omega} = 0.12\\,\\text{A} \\\\ {I}_{3} = \\frac{V}{{R}_{3}} = \\frac{3\\,\\text{V}}{15\\,\\Omega} = 0.20\\,\\text{A} \\end{aligned}\\) As expected, these currents add up to give 0.62 A, which is the total current found going through the equivalent resistor. Also, note that the smallest resistor has the largest current flowing through it, and vice versa."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.ex02","number":"19.3.2","title":"Reasoning with Parallel Resistors","problem":"Without doing any calculation, what is the equivalent resistance of three identical resistors R in parallel?","problemImages":[],"strategy":"Three identical resistors R in parallel make three identical paths through which the current can flow. Thus, it is three times easier for the current to flow through these resistors than to flow through a single one of them.","solution":"If it is three times easier to flow through three identical resistors R than to flow through a single one of them, the equivalent resistance must be three times less: R /3.","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"Let’s check our reasoning by calculating the equivalent resistance of three identical resistors R in parallel. The equation for the equivalent resistance of resistors in parallel gives\n\\(\\begin{aligned} {R}_{\\text{equiv}} = \\frac{1}{1/R + 1/R + 1/R} \\\\ = \\frac{1}{3/R} \\\\ = \\frac{R}{3}\\text{.} \\end{aligned}\\) Thus, our reasoning was correct. In general, when more paths are available through which the current can flow, the equivalent resistance decreases. For example, if we have identical resistors R in parallel, the equivalent resistance would be R /10."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.ex03","number":"19.3.3","title":"Find the Current through a Complex Resistor Circuit","problem":"The battery in the circuit below has a voltage rating of 10 V. What current flows through the circuit and in what direction?","problemImages":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065926806","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit06_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit06_img.jpg","alt":"A circuit diagram showing six resistors, some in series and others parallel."}],"strategy":"Apply the Strategy for finding equivalent resistance to replace all the resistors with a single equivalent resistance, then use Ohm’s law to find the current through the equivalent resistor.","solution":"The resistor combination \\({R}_{4}\\) and \\({R}_{5}\\) can be reduced to an equivalent resistance of\n\\({R}_{\\text{P1}} = \\frac{1}{1/{R}_{4} + 1/{R}_{5}} = \\frac{1}{1/45\\,\\Omega + 1/60\\,\\Omega} = 25.71\\,\\Omega \\text{R.}\\)\nReplacing \\({R}_{4}\\) and \\({R}_{5}\\) with this equivalent resistance gives the circuit below.\nWe now replace the two upper resistors \\({R}_{2}\\) and \\({R}_{3}\\) by the equivalent resistor \\({R}_{\\text{S}1}\\) and the two lower resistors \\({R}_{\\text{P}1}\\) and \\({R}_{6}\\) by their equivalent resistor \\({R}_{\\text{S2}}\\) . These resistors are in series, so we add them together to find the equivalent resistance.\n\\(\\begin{aligned} {R}_{\\text{S1}} = {R}_{2} + {R}_{3} = 50\\,\\Omega + 30\\,\\Omega = 80\\,\\Omega \\\\ {R}_{\\text{S2}} = {R}_{\\text{P1}} + {R}_{6} = 25.71\\,\\Omega + 20\\,\\Omega = 45.71\\,\\Omega \\end{aligned}\\)\nReplacing the relevant resistors with their equivalent resistor gives the circuit below.\nNow replace the two resistors \\({R}_{\\text{S1}}\\text{and}{R}_{\\text{S2}}\\), which are in parallel, with their equivalent resistor \\({R}_{\\text{P2}}\\) . The resistance of \\({R}_{\\text{P2}}\\) is\n\\({R}_{\\text{P2}} = \\frac{1}{1/{R}_{\\text{S1}} + 1/{R}_{\\text{S2}}} = \\frac{1}{1/80\\,\\Omega + 1/45.71\\,\\Omega} = 29.09\\,\\text{Ω.}\\)\nUpdating the circuit diagram by replacing \\({R}_{\\text{S1}}\\text{and}{R}_{\\text{S2}}\\) with this equivalent resistance gives the circuit below.\nFinally, we combine resistors \\({R}_{\\text{1}}\\text{and}{R}_{\\text{P2}}\\), which are in series. The equivalent resistance is \\({R}_{\\text{S3}} = {R}_{1} + {R}_{\\text{P2}} = 75\\,\\Omega + 29.09\\,\\Omega = 104.09\\,\\Omega .\\) The final circuit is shown below.\nWe now use Ohm’s law to find the current through the circuit.\n\\(\\begin{aligned} V = I{R}_{\\text{S3}} \\\\ I = \\frac{V}{{R}_{\\text{S3}}} = \\frac{10\\,\\text{V}}{104.09\\,\\Omega} = 0.096\\,\\text{A} \\end{aligned}\\) The current goes from the positive terminal of the battery to the negative terminal of the battery, so it flows clockwise in this circuit.","solutionImages":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065843526","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit07_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit07_img.jpg","alt":"The same circuit diagram from above but with the two parallel resistors combined."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065883866","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit08_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit08_img.jpg","alt":"The same circuit diagram as above, but with two pairs of resistors in series combined."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167064686256","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit09_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit09_img.jpg","alt":"The same circuit diagram shown above but with the two resistors in series combined."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167062443409","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit10_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit10_img.jpg","alt":"The final circuit diagram showing the final two resistors combined."}],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"This calculation may seem rather long, but with a little practice, you can combine some steps. Note also that extra significant digits were carried through the calculation. Only at the end was the final result rounded to two significant digits."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.ex04","number":"19.3.4","title":"Strange-Looking Circuit Diagrams","problem":"Occasionally, you may encounter circuit diagrams that are not drawn very neatly, such as the diagram shown below. This circuit diagram looks more like how a real circuit might appear on the lab bench. What is the equivalent resistance for the resistors in this diagram, assuming each resistor is 10 \\(\\Omega\\) and the voltage rating of the battery is 12 V.","problemImages":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065973791","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit11_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit11_img.jpg","alt":"A circuit diagram with curved lines between all components instead of straight lines."}],"strategy":"Let’s redraw this circuit diagram to make it clearer. Then we’ll apply the Strategy outlined above to calculate the equivalent resistance.","solution":"To redraw the diagram, consider the figure below. In the upper circuit, the blue resistors constitute a path from the positive terminal of the battery to the negative terminal. In parallel with this circuit are the red resistors, which constitute another path from the positive to negative terminal of the battery. The blue and red paths are shown more cleanly drawn in the lower circuit diagram. Note that, in both the upper and lower circuit diagrams, the blue and red paths connect the positive terminal of the battery to the negative terminal of the battery.\nNow it is easier to see that \\({R}_{\\text{1}}\\text{and}{R}_{2}\\) are in parallel, and the parallel combination is in series with \\({R}_{4}\\) . This combination in turn is in parallel with the series combination of \\({R}_{\\text{3}}\\text{and}{R}_{5}\\) . First, we calculate the blue branch, which contains \\({R}_{1},{R}_{\\text{2}}\\text{, and}{R}_{4}\\) . The equivalent resistance is\n\\({R}_{\\text{blue}} = \\frac{1}{1/{R}_{1} + 1/{R}_{2}} + {R}_{4} = \\frac{1}{1/10\\Omega + 1/10\\,\\Omega} + 10\\,\\Omega = 15\\,\\text{Ω.}\\) where we show the contribution from the parallel combination of resistors and from the series combination of resistors. We now calculate the equivalent resistance of the red branch, which is\n\\({R}_{\\text{red}} = {R}_{3} + {R}_{5} = 10\\,\\Omega + 10\\,\\Omega = 20\\,\\Omega \\text{.}\\)\nInserting these equivalent resistors into the circuit gives the circuit below.\nThese two resistors are in parallel, so they can be replaced by a single equivalent resistor with a resistance of\n\\({R}_{\\text{equiv}} = \\frac{1}{1/{R}_{\\text{blue}} + 1/{R}_{\\text{red}}} = \\frac{1}{1/15\\,\\Omega + 1/20\\,\\Omega} = 8.6\\,\\text{Ω.}\\)\nThe final equivalent circuit is show below.","solutionImages":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065983746","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit12_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit12_img.jpg","alt":"The circuit diagram above redrawn with straight lines. In the upper circuit, the blue resistors constitute a path from the positive terminal of the battery to the negative terminal. In parallel with this circuit are the red resistors, which constitute another path from the positive to negative terminal of the battery."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167064710252","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit13_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit13_img.jpg","alt":"The same circuit diagram but with the red resistors combined and the blue resistors combined."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065938583","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit14_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_circuit14_img.jpg","alt":"The simplified circuit diagram with one resistor equivalent to the original five resistors."}],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"Finding the equivalent resistance was easier with a clear circuit diagram. This is why we try to make clear circuit diagrams, where the resistors in parallel are lined up parallel to each other and at the same horizontal position on the diagram.\nWe can now use Ohm’s law to find the current going through each branch to this circuit. Consider the circuit diagram with \\({R}_{\\text{blue}}\\) and \\({R}_{\\text{red}}\\) . The voltage across each of these branches is 12 V (i.e., the voltage rating of the battery). The current in the blue branch is\n\\({I}_{\\text{blue}} = \\frac{V}{{R}_{\\text{blue}}} = \\frac{12\\,\\text{V}}{15\\,\\Omega} = 0.80\\,\\text{A.}\\)\nThe current across the red branch is\n\\({I}_{\\text{red}} = \\frac{V}{{R}_{\\text{red}}} = \\frac{12\\,\\text{V}}{20\\,\\Omega} = 0.60\\,\\text{A.}\\)\nThe current going through the battery must be the sum of these two currents (can you see why?), or 1.4 A."}],"snapLabs":[],"tipsAndFeatures":[],"summaryPoints":["The equivalent resistance of a group of N identical resistors R connected in parallel is R / N .","Connecting resistors in parallel provides more paths for the current to go through, so the equivalent resistance is always less than the smallest resistance of the parallel resistors.","The same voltage drop occurs across all resistors in parallel; however, the current through each resistor can differ."],"glossary":[{"term":"in parallel","termVi":"Mắc song song","ipa":"/ɪn ˈpær.ə.lel/","definition":"when a group of resistors are connected side by side, with the top ends of the resistors connected together by a wire and the bottom ends connected together by a different wire","definitionVi":"Cách mắc hai đầu các linh kiện chung vào 2 nút → CÙNG hiệu điện thế V!","memoryTipVi":"Mắc song song: V bằng nhau, I cộng lại, R_eq NHỎ HƠN mọi điện trở thành phần!"}],"assessments":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q001","sourceId":"fs-id1167066058038","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"The voltage drop across parallel resistors is ________.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"the same for all resistors"},{"label":"b","text":"greater for the larger resistors"},{"label":"c","text":"less for the larger resistors"},{"label":"d","text":"greater for the smaller resistors"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q002","sourceId":"fs-id1167065067224","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Consider a circuit of parallel resistors. The smallest resistor is 25 Ω . What is the upper limit of the equivalent resistance?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The upper limit of the equivalent resistance is 2.5 Ω."},{"label":"b","text":"The upper limit of the equivalent resistance is 25 Ω."},{"label":"c","text":"The upper limit of the equivalent resistance is 100 Ω."},{"label":"d","text":"There is no upper limit."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q003","sourceId":"fs-id1167064825054","category":"practice-problems","categoryLabel":"Practice Problems (Bài tập vận dụng)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Three resistors, 10, 20, and 30 Ω, are connected in parallel. What is the equivalent resistance?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The equivalent resistance is 5.5 Ω"},{"label":"b","text":"The equivalent resistance is 60 Ω"},{"label":"c","text":"The equivalent resistance is 6 × 103 Ω"},{"label":"d","text":"The equivalent resistance is 6 × 104 Ω"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q005","sourceId":"fs-id1167066044772","category":"practice-problems","categoryLabel":"Practice Problems (Bài tập vận dụng)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the formula for the equivalent resistance of two parallel resistors with resistance R 1 and R 2 ?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Equivalent resistance of two parallel resistors \\({R}_{\\text{eqv}} = {R}_{1} + {R}_{2}\\)"},{"label":"b","text":"Equivalent resistance of two parallel resistors \\({R}_{\\text{eqv}} = {R}_{1}\\, \\times \\,{R}_{2}\\)"},{"label":"c","text":"Equivalent resistance of two parallel resistors \\({R}_{\\text{eqv}} = {R}_{1} - {R}_{2}\\)"},{"label":"d","text":"Equivalent resistance of two parallel resistors \\({R}_{\\text{eqv}} = \\frac{1}{1/{R}_{1} + 1/{R}_{2}}\\)"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q006","sourceId":"fs-id1167064783456","category":"practice-problems","categoryLabel":"Practice Problems (Bài tập vận dụng)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the equivalent resistance for the two resistors shown?","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1164567871354","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex64_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex64_img.jpg","alt":"This shows a vertical parallel circuit. In the middle of either side there are resistors. The left resistor is labeled R1 = 35Ω. The right resistor is labeled R1 = 55Ω"}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The equivalent resistance is 20 Ω"},{"label":"b","text":"The equivalent resistance is 21 Ω"},{"label":"c","text":"The equivalent resistance is 90 Ω"},{"label":"d","text":"The equivalent resistance is 1,925 Ω"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q010","sourceId":"eip-id1166849313816","category":"critical-thinking","categoryLabel":"Critical Thinking (Tư duy phản biện)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the equivalent resistance of the circuit shown below?","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1164564511502","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_equivresi3_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_equivresi3_img.jpg","alt":"The figure shows a horizontal circuit with three resistors. Two horizontal resistors are connected on both ends and labeled 75 ohms and 100 ohms. A third resistor is connected diagonally across the two resistors and labeled 20 ohms."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The equivalent resistance of the circuit 14 Ω."},{"label":"b","text":"The equivalent resistance of the circuit 16.7 Ω."},{"label":"c","text":"The equivalent resistance of the circuit 140 Ω."},{"label":"d","text":"The equivalent resistance of the circuit 195 Ω."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q011","sourceId":"fs-id1167064825054","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Three resistors, 10, 20, and 30 Ω, are connected in parallel. What is the equivalent resistance?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The equivalent resistance is 5.5 Ω"},{"label":"b","text":"The equivalent resistance is 60 Ω"},{"label":"c","text":"The equivalent resistance is 6 × 103 Ω"},{"label":"d","text":"The equivalent resistance is 6 × 104 Ω"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q013","sourceId":"fs-id1167066044772","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the formula for the equivalent resistance of two parallel resistors with resistance R 1 and R 2 ?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Equivalent resistance of two parallel resistors \\({R}_{\\text{eqv}} = {R}_{1} + {R}_{2}\\)"},{"label":"b","text":"Equivalent resistance of two parallel resistors \\({R}_{\\text{eqv}} = {R}_{1}\\, \\times \\,{R}_{2}\\)"},{"label":"c","text":"Equivalent resistance of two parallel resistors \\({R}_{\\text{eqv}} = {R}_{1} - {R}_{2}\\)"},{"label":"d","text":"Equivalent resistance of two parallel resistors \\({R}_{\\text{eqv}} = \\frac{1}{1/{R}_{1} + 1/{R}_{2}}\\)"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q014","sourceId":"fs-id1167064783456","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the equivalent resistance for the two resistors shown?","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1164567871354","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex64_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex64_img.jpg","alt":"This shows a vertical parallel circuit. In the middle of either side there are resistors. The left resistor is labeled R1 = 35Ω. The right resistor is labeled R1 = 55Ω"}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The equivalent resistance is 20 Ω"},{"label":"b","text":"The equivalent resistance is 21 Ω"},{"label":"c","text":"The equivalent resistance is 90 Ω"},{"label":"d","text":"The equivalent resistance is 1,925 Ω"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q015","sourceId":"fs-id1167062361665","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the equivalent resistance of this circuit?","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1164564472101","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex67_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex67_img.jpg","alt":"The figure shows a circuit with three resistors. The first resistor is horizontal and labeled 150 ohms. The left end of the first resistor is connected to a second resistor of 250 ohms. The other end of the second resistor is connected to a third resistor of 50 ohms, which in turn is connected to the right end of the first resistor."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The equivalent resistance of the circuit is 32.7 Ω."},{"label":"b","text":"The equivalent resistance of the circuit is 100 Ω."},{"label":"c","text":"The equivalent resistance of the circuit is 327 Ω."},{"label":"d","text":"The equivalent resistance of the circuit is 450 Ω."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q016","sourceId":"fs-id1167065958602","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the equivalent resistance of the circuit shown below?","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1164567216194","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex68_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex68_img.jpg","alt":"A circuit contains six resistors that are arranged in an outer circuit enclosing an inner circuit. The outer circuit has a 100-ohm horizontal resistor. Left end of the resistor is connected to a second resistor of 50 ohms. The other end of the second resistor is connected to a third resistor of 50 ohms, which in turn is connected to the right end of the first resistor. The inner circuit is identical to the outer circuit, and the left and right ends of both the circuits are connected. In addition, there is a line connecting the points between the two 50-ohm resistors of each circuit."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The equivalent resistance is 25 Ω."},{"label":"b","text":"The equivalent resistance is 50 Ω."},{"label":"c","text":"The equivalent resistance is 75 Ω."},{"label":"d","text":"The equivalent resistance is 100 Ω."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q017","sourceId":"fs-id1167065937918","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"A 10-Ω resistor is connected in parallel to another resistor R . The equivalent resistance of the pair is 8 Ω. What is the resistance R?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"10 Ω"},{"label":"b","text":"20 Ω"},{"label":"c","text":"30 Ω"},{"label":"d","text":"40 Ω"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q018","sourceId":"eip-id1166850248128","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Are the resistors shown connected in parallel or in series? Explain.","images":[{"sourceId":"eip-idm370924928","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_curvycircu_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_curvycircu_img.jpg","alt":"A circuit shows positive terminal of a voltage source connected to one end of a resistor. The other end of the resistor is connected to a second resistor, which is in turn is connected to a third resistor. Finally, the third resistor is connected to the negative terminal of the voltage source."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The resistors are connected in parallel because the same current flows through all three resistors."},{"label":"b","text":"The resistors are connected in parallel because different current flows through all three resistors."},{"label":"c","text":"The resistors are connected in series because the same current flows through all three resistors."},{"label":"d","text":"The resistors are connected in series because different current flows through all three resistors."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q019","sourceId":"fs-id1167064945370","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Ten 100-Ω resistors are connected in series. How can you increase the total resistance of the circuit by about 40 percent?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Adding two 10-Ω resistors increases the total resistance of the circuit by about 40 percent."},{"label":"b","text":"Removing two 10-Ω resistors increases the total resistance of the circuit by about 40 percent."},{"label":"c","text":"Adding four 10-Ω resistors increases the total resistance of the circuit by about 40 percent."},{"label":"d","text":"Removing four 10-Ω resistors increases the total resistance of the circuit by about 40 percent."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec03.q021","sourceId":"fs-id1167065968621","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"In the circuit shown, through which resistor(s) does the most current flow? Through which does the least flow? Explain.","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1169832871769","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex75_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_03_ex75_img.jpg","alt":"A circuit diagram featuring a voltage source and four resistors: 20 Ohm in series, and a parallel combination of 25 Ohm and two 15 Ohm resistors in the remaining branch."}],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The most current flows through the 15-Ω resistor because all the current must pass through this resistor."},{"label":"b","text":"The most current flows through the 20-Ω resistor because all the current must pass through this resistor."},{"label":"c","text":"The most current flows through the 25-Ω resistor because it is the highest resistance."},{"label":"d","text":"The same current flows through the all the resistor because all the current must pass through each of the resistors."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null}],"stats":{"learningObjectiveCount":2,"keyEquationCount":1,"subsectionCount":2,"workedExampleCount":4,"snapLabCount":0,"glossaryCount":1,"assessmentCount":15}},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04","moduleId":"m54446","number":"19.4","slug":"electric-power","title":"Electric Power","isIntroduction":false,"learningObjectives":["Define electric power and describe the electric power equation","Calculate electric power in circuits of resistors in series, parallel, and complex arrangements"],"bilingualGuide":{"notebookSummaryVi":"Công suất tiêu thụ điện (P = IV = I²R = V²/R) và cách tính Điện năng tiêu thụ (kWh).","wordProblemCues":[{"phraseEn":"kilowatt-hour (kWh)","meaningVi":"Số điện năng tiêu thụ (1 kWh = 3.6 MJ)","mathNotation":"E = P · t"}],"commonTrapsVi":["kWh là đơn vị của NĂNG LƯỢNG (Công suất × Thời gian), KHÔNG phải đơn vị của Công suất!"]},"keyEquations":[{"name":"for a given current I flowing through a potential difference V , the electric power dissipated","formula":"\\(P = IV\\)"},{"name":"for a given current I flowing through a resistance R , the electric power dissipated","formula":"\\(P = {I}^{2}R\\)"},{"name":"for a given voltage difference V across a resistor R , the electric power dissipated","formula":"\\(P = \\frac{{V}^{2}}{R}\\)"}],"subsections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.sub01","title":"Performance Task","summaryVi":"Công suất tiêu thụ điện (P = IV = I²R = V²/R) và cách tính Điện năng tiêu thụ (kWh).","paragraphs":[],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167064928581","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_04_PT.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_04_PT.jpg","alt":"A voltage source shows a reading of 6.8 volts. Its position terminal is connected to an ammeter. The other end of the ammeter is connected to a lightbulb, which in turn is connected to the negative terminal of the voltage source."}],"tables":[]}],"workedExamples":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.ex01","number":"19.4.1","title":"Find the Resistance of a Lightbulb","problem":"A typical older incandescent lightbulb was 60 W. Assuming that 120 V is applied across the lightbulb, what is the current through the lightbulb?","problemImages":[],"strategy":"We are given the voltage and the power output of a simple circuit containing a lightbulb, so we can use the equation \\(P = IV\\) to find the current I that flows through the lightbulb.","solution":"Solving \\(P = IV\\) for the current and inserting the given values for voltage and power gives\n\\(\\begin{aligned} P = IV \\\\ I = \\frac{P}{V} = \\frac{60\\,\\text{W}}{120\\,\\text{V}} = 0.50\\,\\text{A.} \\end{aligned}\\)\nThus, a half ampere flows through the lightbulb when 120 V is applied across it.","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"This is a significant current. Recall that household power is AC and not DC, so the 120 V supplied by household sockets is an alternating power, not a constant power. The 120 V is actually the time-averaged power provided by such sockets. Thus, the average current going through the light bulb over a period of time longer than a few seconds is 0.50 A."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.ex02","number":"19.4.2","title":"Boot Warmers","problem":"To warm your boots on cold days, you decide to sew a circuit with some resistors into the insole of your boots. You want 10 W of heat output from the resistors in each insole, and you want to run them from two 9-V batteries (connected in series). What total resistance should you put in each insole?","problemImages":[],"strategy":"We know the desired power and the voltage (18 V, because we have two 9-V batteries connected in series), so we can use the equation \\(P = {V}^{2}/R\\) to find the requisite resistance.","solution":"Solving \\(P = {V}^{2}/R\\) for the resistance and inserting the given voltage and power, we obtain\n\\(\\begin{aligned} P = \\frac{{V}^{2}}{R} \\\\ R = \\frac{{V}^{2}}{P} = \\frac{{(18\\,\\text{V})}^{2}}{10\\,\\text{W}} = 32\\,\\text{Ω.} \\end{aligned}\\)\nThus, the total resistance in each insole should be 32 Ω.","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"Let’s see how much current would run through this circuit. We have 18 V applied across a resistance of 32 \\(\\Omega\\), so Ohm’s law gives\n\\(I = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{18\\,\\text{V}}{32\\,\\Omega} = 0.56\\,\\text{A.}\\)\nAll batteries have labels that say how much charge they can deliver (in terms of a current multiplied by a time). A typical 9-V alkaline battery can deliver a charge of 565 mA ⋅ h\n(so two 9 V batteries deliver 1,130 mA ⋅ h ), so this heating system would function for a time of\n\\(t = \\frac{1130\\, \\times \\,{10}^{-3}\\text{A} \\cdot \\text{h}}{0.56\\,\\text{A}} = 2.0\\,\\text{h.}\\)"},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.ex03","number":"19.4.3","title":"Power through a Branch of a Circuit","problem":"Each resistor in the circuit below is 30 \\(\\Omega\\) . What power is dissipated by the middle branch of the circuit?","problemImages":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167062370208","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_19_04_circuit16_img.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_19_04_circuit16_img.jpg","alt":"A complex circuit diagram with six resistors and multiple branches is shown."}],"strategy":"The middle branch of the circuit contains resistors \\({R}_{\\text{3}}\\text{and}{R}_{5}\\) in series. The voltage across this branch is 12 V. We will first find the equivalent resistance in this branch, and then use \\(P = {V}^{2}/R\\) to find the power dissipated in the branch.","solution":"The equivalent resistance is \\({R}_{\\text{middle}} = {R}_{3} + {R}_{5} = 30\\,\\Omega + 30\\,\\Omega = 60\\,\\Omega\\) . The power dissipated by the middle branch of the circuit is\n\\({P}_{\\text{middle}} = \\frac{{V}^{2}}{{R}_{\\text{middle}}} = \\frac{{(12\\,\\text{V})}^{2}}{60\\,\\Omega} = 2.4\\,\\text{W.}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"Let’s see if energy is conserved in this circuit by comparing the power dissipated in the circuit to the power supplied by the battery. First, the equivalent resistance of the left branch is\n\\({R}_{\\text{left}} = \\frac{1}{1/{R}_{1} + 1/{R}_{2}} + {R}_{4} = \\frac{1}{1/30\\,\\Omega + 1/30\\,\\Omega} + 30\\,\\Omega = 45\\,\\text{Ω.}\\)\nThe power through the left branch is\n\\({P}_{\\text{left}} = \\frac{{V}^{2}}{{R}_{\\text{left}}} = \\frac{{(12\\,\\text{V})}^{2}}{45\\,\\Omega} = 3.2\\,\\text{W.}\\)\nThe right branch contains only \\({R}_{6}\\), so the equivalent resistance is \\({R}_{\\text{right}} = {R}_{6} = 30\\,\\Omega\\) . The power through the right branch is\n\\({P}_{\\text{right}} = \\frac{{V}^{2}}{{R}_{\\text{right}}} = \\frac{{(12\\,\\text{V})}^{2}}{30\\,\\Omega} = 4.8\\,\\text{W.}\\)\nThe total power dissipated by the circuit is the sum of the powers dissipated in each branch.\n\\(P = {P}_{\\text{left}} + {P}_{\\text{middle}} + {P}_{\\text{right}} = 2.4\\,\\text{W} + 3.2\\,\\text{W} + 4.8\\,\\text{W} = 10.4\\,\\text{W}\\)\nThe power provided by the battery is\n\\(P = IV.\\)\nwhere I is the total current flowing through the battery. We must therefore add up the currents going through each branch to obtain I . The branches contributes currents of\n\\(\\begin{aligned} {I}_{\\text{left}} = \\frac{V}{{R}_{\\text{left}}} = \\frac{12\\,\\text{V}}{45\\,\\Omega} = 0.2667\\,\\text{A} \\\\ {\\text{I}}_{\\text{middle}} = \\frac{V}{{R}_{\\text{middle}}} = \\frac{12\\,\\text{V}}{60\\,\\Omega} = 0.20\\,\\text{A} \\\\ {\\text{I}}_{\\text{right}} = \\frac{V}{{R}_{\\text{right}}} = \\frac{12\\,\\text{V}}{30\\,\\Omega} = 0.40\\,\\text{A.} \\end{aligned}\\)\nThe total current is\n\\(I = {I}_{\\text{left}} + {I}_{\\text{middle}} + {I}_{\\text{right}} = 0.2667\\,\\text{A} + 0.20\\,\\text{A} + 0.40\\,\\text{A} = 0.87\\,\\text{A.}\\)\nand the power provided by the battery is\n\\(P = IV = (0.87\\,\\text{A})(12\\,\\text{V}) = 10.4\\,\\text{W.}\\)\nThis is the same power as is dissipated in the resistors of the circuit, which shows that energy is conserved in this circuit."}],"snapLabs":[],"tipsAndFeatures":[],"summaryPoints":["Electric power is dissipated in the resistances of a circuit. Capacitors do not dissipate electric power.","Electric power is proportional to the voltage and the current in a circuit.","Ohm’s law provides two extra expressions for electric power: one that does not involve current and one that does not involve voltage."],"glossary":[{"term":"electric power","termVi":"Công suất điện (P = IV = I²R = V²/R)","ipa":"/ɪˈlek.trɪk ˈpaʊ.ər/","definition":"rate at which electric energy is transferred in a circuit","definitionVi":"Tốc độ tiêu thụ hoặc cung cấp điện năng trong 1 giây (đơn vị Watt, W).","memoryTipVi":"Tiền điện hàng tháng tính theo kWh (1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J — đơn vị ĐIỆN NĂNG, không phải công suất!)."}],"assessments":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q001","sourceId":"fs-id1167064983691","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Which circuit elements dissipate power?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"capacitors"},{"label":"b","text":"inductors"},{"label":"c","text":"ideal switches"},{"label":"d","text":"resistors"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q003","sourceId":"fs-id1167064976820","category":"practice-problems","categoryLabel":"Practice Problems (Bài tập vận dụng)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the formula for the power dissipated in a resistor?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = \\frac{I}{V}\\text{.}\\)"},{"label":"b","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = \\frac{V}{I}\\text{.}\\)"},{"label":"c","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is P = IV ."},{"label":"d","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is P = I 2 V ."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q004","sourceId":"fs-id1167065975455","category":"practice-problems","categoryLabel":"Practice Problems (Bài tập vận dụng)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the formula for power dissipated by a resistor given its resistance and the voltage across it?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = \\frac{R}{{V}^{2}}\\)"},{"label":"b","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = {V}^{2}R\\)"},{"label":"c","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = \\frac{{V}^{2}}{R}\\)"},{"label":"d","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = {I}^{2}R\\)"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q005","sourceId":"fs-id1167064811785","category":"concept","categoryLabel":"Concept Items (Câu hỏi khái niệm)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"To draw the most power from a battery, should you connect a small or a large resistance across its terminals? Explain.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Small resistance, because smaller resistance will lead to the largest power"},{"label":"b","text":"Large resistance, because smaller resistance will lead to the largest power"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q006","sourceId":"eip-id1166849664867","category":"concept","categoryLabel":"Concept Items (Câu hỏi khái niệm)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"If you double the current through a resistor, by what factor does the power dissipated by the resistor change?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Power increases by a factor of two."},{"label":"b","text":"Power increases by a factor of four."},{"label":"c","text":"Power increases by a factor of eight."},{"label":"d","text":"Power increases by a factor of 16."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q007","sourceId":"fs-id1167062401247","category":"critical-thinking","categoryLabel":"Critical Thinking (Tư duy phản biện)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Two lamps have different resistances. (a) If the lamps are connected in parallel, which one is brighter, the lamp with greater resistance or the lamp with less resistance? (b) If the lamps are connected in series, which one is brighter? Note that the brighter lamp dissipates more power.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"(a) lamp with greater resistance; (b) lamp with less resistance"},{"label":"b","text":"(a) lamp with greater resistance; (b) lamp with greater resistance"},{"label":"c","text":"(a) lamp with less resistance; (b) lamp with less resistance"},{"label":"d","text":"(a) lamp with less resistance; (b) lamp with greater resistance"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q008","sourceId":"eip-id1166850929841","category":"critical-thinking","categoryLabel":"Critical Thinking (Tư duy phản biện)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"To measure the power consumed by your laptop computer, you place an ammeter (a device that measures electric current) in series with its DC power supply. When the screen is off, the computer draws 0.40 A of current.\nWhen the screen is on at full brightness, it draws 0.90 A of current. Knowing the DC power supply delivers 16 V, how much power is used by the screen?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The power used by the screen is -8.0 W."},{"label":"b","text":"The power used by the screen is 0.3 W."},{"label":"c","text":"The power used by the screen is 3.2 W."},{"label":"d","text":"The power used by the screen is 8.0 W."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q009","sourceId":"fs-id1167064976820","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the formula for the power dissipated in a resistor?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = \\frac{I}{V}\\text{.}\\)"},{"label":"b","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = \\frac{V}{I}\\text{.}\\)"},{"label":"c","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is P = IV ."},{"label":"d","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is P = I 2 V ."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q010","sourceId":"fs-id1167065975455","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the formula for power dissipated by a resistor given its resistance and the voltage across it?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = \\frac{R}{{V}^{2}}\\)"},{"label":"b","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = {V}^{2}R\\)"},{"label":"c","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = \\frac{{V}^{2}}{R}\\)"},{"label":"d","text":"The formula for the power dissipated in a resistor is \\(P = {I}^{2}R\\)"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q011","sourceId":"fs-id1167066037895","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"When 12 V are applied across a resistor, it dissipates 120 W of power. What is the current through the resistor?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The current is 1,440 A."},{"label":"b","text":"The current is 10 A."},{"label":"c","text":"The current is 0.1 A."},{"label":"d","text":"The current is 0.01 A."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q012","sourceId":"fs-id1167067248945","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Warming 1 g of water requires 4.18 J of energy per °C. How long would it take to warm 1 L of water from 20 to 40 °C\nif you immerse in the water a 1-kW resistor connected across a 9.0-V battery?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"10 min"},{"label":"b","text":"20 min"},{"label":"c","text":"30 min"},{"label":"d","text":"40 min"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q016","sourceId":"eip-id1166849161730","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What power is dissipated in a circuit through which 0.12 A flows across a potential drop of 3.0 V?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"0.36 W"},{"label":"b","text":"0.011 W"},{"label":"c","text":"5 V"},{"label":"d","text":"2.5 W"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q017","sourceId":"fs-id1167063178156","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"You want to increase the power dissipated in a circuit. You have the choice between doubling the current or doubling the resistance, with the voltage remaining constant. Which one would you choose?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"doubling the resistance"},{"label":"b","text":"doubling the current"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q018","sourceId":"fs-id1167062469196","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"You want to increase the power dissipated in a circuit. You have the choice between reducing the voltage or reducing the resistance, with the current remaining constant. Which one would you choose?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"reduce the voltage to increase the power"},{"label":"b","text":"reduce the resistance to increase the power"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q019","sourceId":"fs-id1167065955581","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What power is dissipated in the circuit consisting of 310-Ω resistors connected in series across a 9.0-V battery?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The power dissipated is 2430 W."},{"label":"b","text":"The power dissipated is 270 W."},{"label":"c","text":"The power dissipated is 2.7 W."},{"label":"d","text":"The power dissipated is 0.37 W."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch19.sec04.q020","sourceId":"fs-id1167064979699","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What power is dissipated in a circuit consisting of three 10-Ω resistors connected in parallel across a 9.0-V battery?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The power dissipated is 270 W."},{"label":"b","text":"The power dissipated is 30 W."},{"label":"c","text":"The power dissipated is 24 W."},{"label":"d","text":"The power dissipated is 1/24 W."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null}],"stats":{"learningObjectiveCount":2,"keyEquationCount":3,"subsectionCount":1,"workedExampleCount":3,"snapLabCount":0,"glossaryCount":1,"assessmentCount":16}}],"summary":{"totalSections":5,"totalKeyEquations":5,"totalWorkedExamples":13,"totalSnapLabs":1,"totalGlossaryTerms":17,"totalAssessments":53,"referencedMediaCount":48},"qualityGate":{"passed":true,"checkedUids":80,"errors":[],"warnings":[]}};
